based on the second search, we can conclude that someone left the theater with or wearing Monte Field's hat that night, so he must have left his own hat in the theater. [2149] \"Unless he and the other audience members were allowed to leave and slipped away, he couldn't have taken the hat out of the theater; because all the exits were guarded or locked at that time. The left inner alley had Jesse Lynch and Eleanor Libby blocking it, followed by usher John Tice watching, and then one of our police officers guarding. There was no exit on the right, so the hat couldn't be taken from there. [2150] \"Following this line of thought - because Field's hat was a top hat, and no one leaves a theater wearing a top hat without a full evening gown - we observed this very carefully - so whoever took the\n[2151] \"It seems very logical,\" Sampson admitted, \"although it is quite complex.\" [2152] \"You have no idea how complicated this is,\" the officer said solemnly, \"because we must remember other possibilities - for example, the person who took Field's hat is not the killer, but an accomplice. Let's continue. [2153] \"The next question we ask ourselves is: What happened to the high top hat left by the murderer in the theater? How did he dispose of it? Where did he put it?... I can tell you, it's a puzzle. We searched the place up and down. Yes, we found some hats in the backstage, and Mrs. Philip, the custodian, confirmed that they were personal belongings of the actors, but none of them belonged to them privately. So, where could the high top hat left by the murderer be? Ellery hit the truth with his sharp mind. He muttered to himself: 'The murderer's hat must be here. We didn't find any unusual or unremarkable high top hats, so the high top hats we searched must not be unremarkable.' That's the crux of the matter, isn't it? Simple to the point of absurdity, yet I didn't think of it.\" [2154] \"What kind of hat is this? - An ordinary hat in an ordinary place, so unremarkable that no one questioned it. At the Roman Theater, all costumes were rented from Le Brun, so the answer is simple: it's a rented hat for the performance. Where would these hats be? Either in the men's dressing room or in the wardrobe room where all the actors' costumes are stored. When Ellery reasoned this out, he took Mrs. Philip to the backstage, checked every hat in the men's dressing room and the wardrobe room. Every single hat there \u2013 all of them accounted for, none missing \u2013 was a prop hat with Le Brun's label on the lining. And Field's hat, we had all confirmed, was a \"Brown Brothers\" derby, not among the props, and not anywhere backstage.\" [2155] \"Since no one left the theater wearing more than one hat on Monday night, and since Field's hat was undoubtedly taken the same evening, it can be concluded that the murderer's own top hat must have been in the Roman Theater during the closure period and was still there when we conducted our second search. Now, the hats left in the theater are all props. Therefore, the next inference is that the murderer left wearing Field's hat, so his own hat must have been one of the props in the backstage. So, to reiterate, these prop hats are the only actual top hats that exist.\" [2156] \"In other words - one of the props backstage belonged to the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2157] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2158] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2159] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2160] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2161] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2162] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of