 Field, [2183] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2184] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2185] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2186] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2187] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2188] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2189] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2190] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2191] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2192] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2193] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2194] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2195] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2196] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2197] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2198]\n[2157] \"If this person is the killer \u2013 it couldn't be anyone else \u2013 then our search area is greatly reduced. He could only be one of the male actors wearing evening attire who left, or someone closely associated with the theater and dressed similarly. This person would first need to have a prop hat that must be left behind; secondly, have access to the costume storage room and dressing room without being detected; and finally, have the opportunity to leave his prop hat in the costume storage room and dressing room. [2158] \"Now let's analyze the possibility of the second scenario - the killer is closely related to the theater but not an actor.\" The policeman paused, taking a deep breath of his cherished snuff, \"The backstage staff can be ruled out as none of them wore evening gowns, which are necessary to take away Field's high hat. Similarly, the cashier, usher, doorman, and other minor staff can also be ruled out. The promoter Harry Nelson was dressed in ordinary civilian clothes. Manager Poulson wore a full suit, yes, but I measured his hat size and found it to be six and three-quarters - an unusually small size. He couldn't wear Field's hat, which was seven and three-quarters. We left the theater before him, that's right. But on my way out, I specifically instructed Thomas Wiley to search Poulson as thoroughly as he would search others. Earlier that evening, I checked Poulson's hat in his office out of a sense of responsibility and found it to be a round top hat. Wiley later reported that Poulson left with this top hat, without taking any other hats. Now - if Poulson were the person we were looking for, he might have left with Field's hat, although it was too big for him to wear, but he could have carried it. However, he left with a top hat, so there is no doubt that he could not have taken Field's hat, as the door was locked when he left the theater, and no one - I can guarantee with my staff on duty - entered the theater until Thursday morning when I opened the door myself. Theoretically, anyone who could hide Field's hat in the theater, such as Poulson or any other employee, could be the killer. But this last assumption was refuted by the report of our official building expert, Edmund Crow. He clearly stated that there are no secret hiding places in the Roman Theater. [2159] \"Having eliminated Parelli, Nelsen, and the staff, there was only the possibility of the actors left. I'll leave it to you to guess how we narrowed down the field to Barry. The most intriguing part of this case, in fact, was a series of surprising and intricate deductions that led to the truth. I should say 'we' \u2014 but really, I should say 'Eliot'\u2026\"\n[2160] \"As a detective, you are quite the bashful one,\" Crooning chuckled, \"Oh, this is more thrilling than a detective story. I should be working now, but my boss seems equally interested \u2013 go on, officer!\" [2161] Quinn smiled and continued speaking. [2162] \"The fact that the killer's identity was traced to an actor,\" he continued, \"answered a question that may have occurred to you and has been bothering us from the start. At first, we didn't understand why the theater was chosen as the meeting place for the secret deal. You stop to think, and in general, the theater has many inconveniences. Just one thing: to ensure privacy, you have to buy several extra tickets around the seats to make them empty. Compared to other more convenient meeting places, choosing such a chaotic place is simply ridiculous! The theater is mostly dark and quiet, which is unsettling. Any unexpected sound or conversation would be noticed. The audience is also a potential danger \u2013 they could be recognized. However, when you realize that Barry is an actor, all these problems are solved. From his perspective, the theater is ideal \u2013 because when the victim is found dead in the front row of the stalls, who would suspect the actor as the killer? Of course, Field consented to this arrangement, not realizing what Barry was thinking, and not realizing that he was conspiring in his own death. You must remember, he dealt with dangerous people all the time, even if he had some suspicions, he thought he could handle it. This might have made him a little too confident \u2013 of course, we'll never know. [2163] \"Let's go back to Ellery - my favorite topic,\" the detective continued, chuckling as usual, \"Besides these deductions about the hat - actually, before these deductions appeared - Ellery had noticed suspicious signs at the Evans-Pope house during their meeting. It was clear that during the intermission, Field had approached Frances Evans-Pope in the alley not just for flirting. In Ellery's view, there was some kind of connection between these two distant individuals. However, this did not mean that Frances had to know about this connection. She was absolutely certain that she had never heard of or seen Field before. We have no reason to suspect her, but we have ample reason to believe her.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf Stephen Barry and Field knew each other, and Frances was unaware, the possible link might be Stephen Barry. For example, if Field had an appointment with the actor on Monday evening and suddenly saw Frances, he might have dared to approach her, especially if the topic between him and Barry was related to her. As for why she recognized her - thousands of people who read the daily newspaper are familiar with her face - she is a young socialite who is often photographed. Field, with his comprehensive business methods, naturally knew her appearance and appearance... But let's go back to this triangle relationship - Field, Frances, Barry - I will explain in detail later. Through photos and other news channels, everyone knows that Barry is engaged to Frances and is her fianc\u00e9. You must have realized that, in this case, among the actors, no one else can answer this question: why did Field approach Frances? [2164] \"There is another puzzling factor about Frances - her purse was found in Field's clothes - a reasonable explanation is that, when the inebriated lawyer approached her, she dropped it out of nervousness. Later, Jesse Lynch's testimony also corroborated this, as his testimony suggested that he saw Field pick up Frances' purse. Poor girl - I feel sorry for her.\" The officer sighed. [2165] \"Let's go back to the hat\u2014you'll notice we've always been mentioning that damn hat.\" Quin paused, then continued, \"I've never seen a case where a single factor has played a part in every aspect of an investigation\u2026Now, pay attention to this: out of all the cast members, Barry was the only one who left the Roman Theater on Monday night wearing a tuxedo and a top hat. On Monday night, Ellery noticed as people were leaving the theater that everyone else in the cast was dressed in casual attire; in fact, later, when he was in Pencroft's office, he mentioned this to Sampson and me, but we didn't fully realize its significance at the time\u2026Therefore, Barry was the only cast member who could have taken Field's hat. By using Ellery's reasoning about the hat, we can now cut through the fog and conclude that Barry is the suspect. [2166] \"The next step we need to do is to see the performance ourselves. That evening - Thursday, Ellery made a decisive inference. You'll understand why. We wanted to see if Barry had time to commit the crime during the second act to confirm our conclusion. It was surprising that out of all the cast members, only Barry had the time. He left the stage at 9:20 - he went on stage, and then left almost immediately - and didn't return until 9:50, staying on stage until the act ended. This was an undeniable part of the fixed schedule. The other actors were either on stage throughout or had very short intervals between going on and off stage. This means that on Thursday night, which was five days ago, we solved the mystery - the whole case took nine days to solve, but figuring out the identity of the killer and bringing him to justice were two different things. You'll understand in a moment.\" [2167] \"The killer wouldn't be able to enter until around 9:30, which explains why the edges torn off of LL32 left and LL30 left are not consistent. You know, Field and Barry had to come in at different times. Field wouldn't be able to come in with Barry, or even be too obviously late \u2013 it was too important for Barry to keep the secret, and Field understood, or he thought he understood the necessity of adhering to the rules of secrecy.\" [2168] \"When we determined Barry to be a suspect on Thursday evening, we decided to subtly interrogate the other members of the cast and the staff behind the scenes. Of course, we wanted to find out if anyone had truly seen Barry leave or return. Coincidentally, no one had. Everyone was either busy with the performance, changing costumes, or working backstage. This investigation was conducted after the show ended that night and Barry had left the theater. This approach turned out to be quite effective. [2169] \"We borrowed a seating chart from Poulson. With this chart, along with a quick check of the left aisle after the second act on Thursday night, and the layout of the dressing rooms on stage \u2013 we can figure out how the killer did it.\" [2170] Sampson was visibly excited. \"I've been wrestling with this for quite some time,\" he admitted, \"after all, Field isn't a fool. This Barry must be a genius, Kwai, how did he do it?\" [2171] \"Once the mystery is unraveled, any riddle is simple,\" the detective answered. \"After 9:20, Barry had free time, so he quickly went back to the dressing room, did a thorough disguise on his face, put on a cloak, and a high hat, which was part of his costume - you all remember, he was wearing evening dress - and then slipped out of the dressing room and into the alley. Of course, you don't know the layout of the theater. There are many floor levels facing the left alley on the backstage side of the theater, which are dressing rooms. Barry's dressing room was on the ground floor, with a door facing the alley. There is an iron staircase leading down to the sidewalk. [2172] \"He left the dressing room through this door, slipped through the dark alley when the side door was closed during the second act. He sneaked out onto the street when no one was guarding the end of the alley - luckily for him, Jesse Lynch and his 'girl' hadn't arrived yet. Then he walked boldly into the theater as if he were late. He showed his ticket at the door - LL30 left, wrapped in a cloak, of course disguised very well. He entered the theater and deliberately threw away the stub. This was a wise move on his part, as he thought that if they found the stub there, it would only arouse suspicion in a certain audience member, not on the stage. Moreover, if his plan failed, he would be searched thoroughly later, and the stub found on him would be irrefutable evidence. Ultimately, he thought his actions would not only divert attention but also protect him. [2173] \"But how did he find his seat without a usher? If there had been an usher, he would have been seen. \"The detective answered, \"He didn't intend to avoid the usher. Since the performance was going on, the theater was dark, and he naturally hoped that the usher hadn't arrived yet and sat in the last row closest to the door. Even if the usher arrived before him and led him to his seat, with his disguise and the darkness of the theater, he wouldn't be recognized. Therefore, in the worst case, others would only remember that a man came in during the second act of the performance, and even his general outline would be difficult to describe. Coincidentally, no one approached him because Maggie O'Connell was luckily sitting next to her sweetheart. So he could sit next to Field without being noticed.\" [2174] \"But how did he find his seat without an usher? If there had been an usher, he would have been seen.\" Croaning disbelievingly. [2175] \"He had no intention of avoiding the usher,\" the officer replied, \"since it was during the performance and the theater was dimly lit, he naturally hoped the usher had not yet arrived and was seated in the back row closest to the entrance. However, even if the usher arrived before him and led him to his seat, due to his good disguise and the dark theater, he would not be recognized. Therefore, in the worst-case scenario, others' recollections of him would be of a man who entered during the second act, with a body outline that was barely describable. Coincidentally, no one approached him because Marcie O'Connell was fortunately sitting next to her sweetheart. Thus, he was able to sit quietly next to Field without being noticed. [2176] \"Please remember, what I just said is not a deduction or investigation result. We don't have the ability to discover these facts. Barry's confession last night revealed these details... Once we knew the criminal was Barry, we could deduce the whole process... If you know the criminal, it's simple and normal. However, this is not necessary for solving the case. Does this sound like an excuse for Ellery or me? Hmmph!\" The old man forced a smile. [2177] \"He had a well-planned plan when he sat next to Field,\" the officer cleared his throat, continuing. \"He had to strictly follow the schedule and not waste a minute. Moreover, Field knew that Barry had to return, so he didn't delay. Barry told us that he thought it would be difficult to deal with Field, but it wasn't as difficult as he thought. Field was amiable and accepted Barry's suggestion, perhaps because he had drunk too much and wanted to get that large sum of money quickly. [2178] \"Barry first asked for the documents. Field cunningly demanded money before showing the documents, and Barry showed him a wallet that was obviously full of real bills. The theater was very dark, and Barry didn't take out the bills. In fact, these were the bills used in the performance on stage. Barry hinted at the bills, but he wanted to check the documents first before paying. Field was probably expecting this. We know that Barry is a successful actor, and his training on stage allows him to handle difficult situations with ease...\n[2179] \"Imagine his reaction when Field reached under his seat and pulled out his high hat. Barry confessed that at the time, Field said, 'Didn't you expect me to hide the documents here? In fact, I specifically use this hat to store your records. Look - here is your name.' He said this while flipping the hatband over! Barry saw his name written in ink on the inside of the hatband with his flashlight. [2180] \"Imagine his brain's reaction at that moment. His meticulous plan was about to be ruined by this hat. If his hat had been checked when his body was found - of course, it would have been checked - the name 'Stephen Barry' on the hatband would have been the ironclad evidence... Barry didn't have time to remove the hatband. First, he didn't have a knife - unfortunately\n[2181] \"Sensitive mind, Henry, sensitive mind,\" Quinn said gravely, \"many have gone to the gallows... just as he was about to take the hat, he realized he couldn't leave his own there. For one, his hat was a fedora \u2013 a foldable, wide-brimmed hat \u2013 but more importantly, it bore the name of the costume supplier, L\u00e9on Broun. You can see, this would immediately point to someone in the production \u2013 which was exactly what he wanted to avoid. He told me, at the time and for some time after, he thought the police could only infer that something valuable was hidden in the hat. He couldn't fathom how this line of investigation would implicate him. When I explained to him that Ellery simply made deductions based on the fact of the lost hat, he was completely astounded... Now you understand, his only fundamental flaw in the commission of the crime was not negligence or error, but the unforeseen occurrence that forced him to act, which in turn set off a chain reaction. If Barry's name hadn't been on Field's hat, I wouldn't have had any suspicions, and until today, he would have been a free man with no suspicion. The police files would have added another unsolved murder case. [2182] \"As you can imagine, these thoughts flashed through his mind in a moment, not as long as I've described. He knew what he had to do, and his plans had to be adjusted immediately due to the new developments... After Field retrieved the documents from the hat, Barry quickly checked them under the watchful gaze of the lawyer. He used the same mini flashlight, which was dimmed due to the obstruction from the two of them. The documents appeared to be well-organized and complete. However, Barry didn't spend much time on them at that moment. He lifted his head and said, \"Seems like everything's here, damn it\u2014\" in a natural tone, as if they were already reconciled enemies, and he was a magnanimous person. Field didn't understand the true meaning of this statement. Barry reached into his pocket \u2013 the flashlight was off at that time \u2013 and appeared to be nervous, taking a gulp of the premium whisky from the bottle. He then seemed to realize he was being impolite and asked Field if he would like to take a sip to celebrate the successful transaction. Seeing Barry drink from the bottle, Field no longer had any suspicions. In fact, he probably never imagined that Barry would try to eliminate him. Barry handed him the bottle...\n[2183] \"But it wasn't the same bottle. Under the cover of darkness, he took out two bottles - the one he drank from was taken out of his left back pocket, while the one he gave to Field was taken out of his right back pocket. It was simple - it was dark there, and the lawyer was already drunk, so it was even simpler. The bottle trick worked. But Barry was thorough. He had a syringe filled with poison in his pocket. If Field refused to drink, Barry was prepared to inject the needle into Field's arm or thigh. He had an injection needle given to him by a doctor years ago. Barry had suffered from neurasthenia but could not undergo normal treatment by the doctor because he had to perform with the troupe in various places. Since the syringe was old, its origin could not be traced; Barry was also prepared if Field did not drink. You see - even in such details, his plan was flawless...\"\n[2184] \"The bottle of Scotch that Field drank contained high-quality whiskey, that's true, but it was heavily laced with tetraethyl lead. The strong smell of the liquor masked the subtle ether taste of the poison; Field had taken a large gulp before realizing something was amiss - if he had realized at all. [2185] \"He handed the bottle back to Barry without a second thought, and Barry pocketed it, saying, 'I think I need to look over these documents more closely - I don't trust you, Field...' Field was by now so dull-witted that he simply nodded his head blankly, then collapsed onto his seat. Barry truly examined the documents, but all the while kept an eagle's eye on Field with the corner of his eye. After about five minutes, he saw that Field was finished - completely finished. He wasn't completely unconscious, but he was close; his face contorted, and he couldn't breathe. He seemed unable to move or cry out. Of course, in his agony, he forgot all about Barry, and soon he was completely unconscious. When he spoke his last words to Basker, it was merely the dying flicker of a flame...\n[2186] \"Barry looked at his watch. It was 9:40, he had only been with Field for ten minutes. He had to be back on stage at 9:50. He decided to wait another three minutes - this took less time than he had anticipated - to ensure that Field wouldn't make any more noise. At 9:43, Field was in agony and on the brink of death, Barry picked up Field's hat, quickly removed his own hat and tucked it under his cloak, stood up. He was familiar with the route. He stuck to the wall, carefully and inconspicuously making his way along the aisle to the back of the left box. The play was at its climax, and everyone was focused on the stage, none of them noticing him. [2187] \"Behind the box, he tore off the wig, quickly adjusted his makeup, and walked through the stage door. This door led to a narrow passage, which led to a corridor, which then turned into different areas of the stage area. His dressing room was only a few feet away from the entrance of the corridor. He slipped in, tossing his prop hat into his ordinary items, pouring the remaining poison from the bottle into the sink, and washing the bottle. He poured the remaining liquid from the syringe into the toilet, and put away the cleaned needle. If he was caught - what did it matter? He had a valid reason for having the syringe, and besides, he hadn't used it in the murder case... He was now ready to go on stage, calm, happy, and confident, but also slightly annoyed. At exactly 9:50, he was called to the stage. He walked up to the stage, and at 9:55, there was a shout from the front row of the auditorium... \"\n[2188] \"Discuss your complex plans,\" said Sampson suddenly. [2189] \"It's not as complicated as it first sounded,\" the officer replied, \"Remember, Barry is a very intelligent and accomplished young man, especially an excellent actor. Only an experienced actor could pull off a plan like this. After all, the process is simple; the hardest part is sticking to the schedule. If he was seen, he would have been disguised. The most dangerous part of his plan was \u2013 how to escape when he walked down the aisle, through the backstage entrance to the stage, while the ushers were there. He had observed the ushers while sitting next to Field. Of course, he knew that due to the nature of the performance, the ushers would be mostly faithful to their duties, and he counted on his disguise and syringe to help him through any unexpected situations. However, Maggie O'Connell did not do her job properly, so that was to his advantage. He proudly told me last night that he had prepared for emergencies... As for the backstage entrance, he knew from experience that everyone would be on stage and the technicians would be busy in their positions at that time... Remember, he planned the crime with the exact conditions in mind. If there were any dangers and uncertainties \u2013 well, isn't that the nature of an adventure? \u2013 he asked me with a smile last night; I had to admit that, if not for this, I would greatly admire his style.\" [2190] The officer uneasily shifted the topic. \"I hope I've made it clear how Barry committed the crime. As for our investigation... besides the reasoning about the hat and knowing the identity of the killer, we still know nothing about the exact details of the crime. If you remember the evidence we gathered on Thursday night, you will understand that there was nothing to go on. What we hope most is that there is some clue in the files we need to find that is related to Barry. Even then, it's not enough... so the next step, the officer sighed, \"is to find the secret compartment on the canopy of the bed in Field's apartment. This work has been done by Ellery from beginning to end. We found that Field did not have a safe, no mailbox, no other residence, no intimate neighbors or business partners, and those files were not in his office. After elimination, Ellery insisted that these files were in Field's room. You know how the search ended - completely through Ellery's genius reasoning. We found Morgan's files, discovered the material needed by Croning about the mafia activities - by the way, Tim, I keenly noticed what happened when we started the big clean-up - we finally found a bundle of miscellaneous files, including Michaels and Barry's... Tim, you will remember, Ellery deduced from handwriting analysis that we might find Barry's original files - and indeed we did. [2191] \"Mickles' case was quite interesting.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[548] \"That's good,\" Quine approved. \"So, when was the last time you saw Monty Field?\" [549] Morgan cleared his throat uneasily. \"Er... I haven't seen him in a while,\" he whispered in response. \"You see, we used to be partners - successful in legal matters. Then some things happened, and we went our separate ways. Since then, I... I haven't seen him again.\" [550] \"How long ago was that?\" [551] \"About two years and a bit.\" [552] \"Very good.\" Quinn leaned forward. \"I also want to know why you split up.\" [553] Morgan looked down at the carpet, fiddling with his cigar. \"I... uh, I guess you know what kind of person Field is. We had a disagreement about ethics, and after a small argument, we decided to go our separate ways.\" [554] \"Did you part on good terms?\" [555] \"Uh... in that situation, yes.\" [556] Quinn tapped the table. Morgan shifted uncomfortably. He clearly hadn't fully recovered from the shock. [557] \"What time did you arrive at the theater tonight, Morgan?\" the officer asked. [558] Morgan seemed surprised by the question. \"Ah\u2014around 8:15,\" he replied. [559] \"Let me see your ticket stub,\" Quinn said. [560] Morgan fumbled through his pockets before producing it and handing it to Quinn. Quinn took the ticket stub, then pulled out three more from his pockets, placing them under the table and carefully comparing them. He then returned the four ticket stubs to his pocket without expression. [561] \"So, you were sitting in the middle M2 seat, right?\" he said. \"Nice spot, Morgan. What made you want to see 'Gunplay'?\" [562] \"Ah, it's a novelty show, isn't it, officer?\" Morgan looked a bit embarrassed. \"I didn't even know I'd want to come to the theater \u2013 you know, I don't usually go to the theater \u2013 but the manager of the Roman Theater sent me a free ticket for tonight's performance, so I came.\" [563] \"Is that so?\" Quine asked bluntly. \"When did you receive the ticket?\" [564] \"Ah, I got the letter on Saturday morning, officer, delivered to my office.\" [565] \"Oh, you got a letter too, did you? You don't happen to have it on you, do you?\" [566] \"I\u2014very\u2014sure\u2014I brought it,\" he mumbled, starting to rummage through his pockets, \"Here it is! Here you go.\" [567] He handed the officer a small rectangular piece of rough-edged paper made of wrinkled securities printing paper. Officer Quinn carefully took it to the light. Through the printed lines, a clear watermark pattern could be seen. He pursed his lips and carefully placed the paper on the desk's notepad. Under Morgan's gaze, he opened the drawer above Posen's desk and searched until he found a sheet of paper. The paper was large and square, with a luxurious theater seal printed on it. Quinn placed the two papers side by side, pondered for a moment, then sighed, picked up the one Morgan handed him, and read it slowly. [568] The Roman Theater Department cordially invites Benjamin Morgan, Esq. to attend the performance of 'Gunfight' on September 24th (Monday) evening. We sincerely hope that Mr. Morgan, a leading figure in the legal profession in New York, will share his valuable opinions on the social and legal aspects of the play. Of course, we do not impose any obligation. The Roman Theater Department guarantees that Mr. Morgan can accept this invitation without any obligation. [569] (Signature) Roman Theater\n[570] Inviter: S.\n[571] The \"S\" written in ink was sloppy and almost unrecognizable. [572] Quin looked up, smiled. \"Roman Theatre is quite something, Mr. Morgan. I was wondering -\" he continued to smile, gesturing towards Johnson, who had been sitting quietly in the corner, listening to their conversation. [573] \"Call the manager, Johnson,\" Quin said, \"if that publicity man - that Bellson, or Pearson, or whoever - is here, have him come too.\" [574] After Johnson left, Quin turned back to Morgan. [575] \"Could you show me your gloves, Mr. Morgan?\" he whispered. [576] Morgan looked at him perplexed and then threw the gloves on the table in front of him. Quinn picked them up curiously. They were a pair of white silk gloves \u2014 typically worn with evening attire. Quinn pretended to be busy examining the gloves. He turned them over, closely inspecting a smudge on one fingertip, and even tried to put them on his own hand, joking with Morgan. After his examination, he returned the gloves to Morgan with a serious expression. [577] \"Ah \u2014 oh, by the way, Mr. Morgan \u2014 your hat is quite a sight. May I see it?\" [578] Morgan placed the hat on the table, still silent. Quinn picked it up nonchalantly, whistling a tune called \"Broadway,\" a few notes lower than standard pitch. He flipped the hat in his hand. It was a high-quality hat, with a lustrous silk lining and a gold stamp of the manufacturer, \"James Chalmers & Co.\" The initials \"B.M.\" were embroidered on the band. [579] Quinn grinned and put the hat on his own head. It fit perfectly. But he quickly took it off and handed it back to Morgan. [580] \"Thank you very much for allowing me to examine your gloves and hat, Mr. Morgan,\" he said as he pulled out a notepad from his pocket and jotted down a note hastily. [581] The door opened, and Johnson, Pensel, and Harry Nelson entered. Pensel hesitated before approaching, while Nelson sat in an armchair. [582] \"What can I do for you, officer?\" Pensel asked nervously, trying to ignore the dejected, silver-haired figure sitting in the chair. [583] \"Mr. Pensel,\" Quinn said calmly, \"In your Roman Theatre, how many kinds of writing paper do you have?\" [584] The manager's eyes widened. \"Just one, officer. There's one right in front of you on the table.\" [585] \"Ah \u2013\" Quinn handed Pensel the paper he had taken from Morgan. \"I want you to take a good look at this paper, Mr. Pensel. Do you know if the Roman Theatre has such paper?\" [586] The manager gave the paper a strange look. \"No, I don't think we have such paper. In fact, I'm quite sure. What does it say?\" He turned around and called out, \"Nelson! What is this?\u2014Is it your latest promotional gimmick?\" He waved the paper in front of Nelson. [587] Nelson took the paper from the manager and quickly read it. \"Ah, it seems I'll have to change careers,\" he whispered. \"This is bound to break the advertising record.\" He read it again, his face showing envy. Then, under the gaze of four reproachful eyes, he handed the paper back to Pencier. \"I'm sorry, such a brilliant idea didn't even occur to me,\" he said slowly, retreating to the corner and crossing his arms. [588] The manager turned to Quine with a puzzled expression. \"This is strange, officer. As far as I know, the Roman Theater never uses such stationery, and I'm quite certain I never authorized anyone to do such promotional work. If Nelson says he didn't do it\u2014\"\n[589] Quine carefully put the paper in his pocket. \"Alright, thank you, gentlemen,\" he said, nodding at Pencier and Nelson as they left. [590] He looked at Morgan, who was flushed with anger and embarrassment. Quine raised his hand and slammed it lightly on the table again. [591] \"What do you think of this, Mr. Morgan?\" He asked straightforwardly. [592] Morgan jumped up. \"Damn it, this is a frame-up!\" He waved his fist in front of Quinn, \"Please excuse my rudeness, I know no more about this than you do. And don't think you can scare me by searching my hat and gloves - oh, you haven't searched my underwear yet, Inspector!\" [593] \"But my dear Morgan,\" Inspector Quinn said gently, \"why do you have to make yourself so nervous? Others would think I accused you of murdering Monte Field. Sit down, calm down, old boy. I'll ask you a simple question.\" [594] Morgan slumped in the chair, wiped his forehead with a trembling hand, and whispered, \"Sorry, Inspector, I couldn't control my temper. But these unfortunate things -\" He calmed down and murmured. [595] Inspector Quinn sat there, staring at him with a puzzled look. Morgan was busy wiping his sweat with a handkerchief and puffing on a cigar. Johnson coughed disapprovingly and looked up at the ceiling. Suddenly, there was a commotion in the room, but it soon quieted down. [596] Quin's voice broke the silence. \"Well, that's all, Mr. Morgan. You can go now.\" [597] Morgan slowly rose, opened his mouth to say something, but closed it again, put on his hat, and left the room. Johnson saw the officer's gesture and lazily stood up, casually opening the door for him. Both men left. [598] The room was left with Quin alone, who immediately got to work. He pulled out four ticket stubs, the letter Morgan had given him, and the lady's rhinestone handbag he had found in the dead man's pocket. He opened the handbag again, took out its contents, and laid them on the table in front of him. There were several business cards with \"Frances Eves-Popp\" printed elegantly; two delicate handkerchiefs; a small makeup case containing foundation, blush, and lipstick; a small money pouch containing twenty-dollar bills and some coins; and a door key. Quin toyed with these items for a moment, put them back in the handbag, and put the handbag, ticket stubs, and letter back in his pocket. He stood up, slowly scanning the room, then walked over to the coat rack, picked up a top hat hanging there, and checked its lining. It had the initials \"L.P.\" printed on it, and the hat size was 6 3/4, which piqued his interest. [599] He put the hat back in its place, opened the door. [600] The four people sitting in the reception room immediately stood up, their faces expressing relief. Quin stood at the door, smiling, with his hands in his coat pockets. [601] \"It's finally your turn,\" he said. \"Come on, everyone, come into the office.\" [602] He politely stepped aside to let them go first \u2013 three ladies and a young man. They all entered the office with some excitement, the young man hurrying to pull chairs for the ladies to sit down. Four pairs of eyes eagerly looked at the elderly man standing at the door. Quein smiled, like a fatherly figure. He quickly glanced at the reception room, then closed the door, and sternly walked to the table and sat down, reaching for his snuffbox. [603] \"Alright!\" he said mildly. \"I'm very sorry to have kept you waiting so long \u2013 official business, you know. Now, let's get started. Well, right, right, I must \u2013 well, now, first of all, ladies and this gentleman, how shall we begin? I believe, young lady, although I haven't had the pleasure of hearing you introduce yourself, your name should be Frances Eves-Pope, is that right?\" [604] The girl raised her eyebrows. \"Yes, sir,\" her voice was lively and melodious, \"although I'm not sure how you know my name.\" [605] She smiled. Her smile was attractive and seductive, and her gaze was captivating. She was in her prime, graceful and charming, with brown, large eyes and a complexion like jade. She exuded vitality that revitalized Quein's spirits. [606] He smiled at her. \"Well, Miss Eves-Pope, I think for most people, your identity would be a mystery. But I'm a policeman, so I'm stronger than most people, so it's not difficult for me to know your name. And you're definitely not an unknown girl, in fact, I saw your photo in the social section of the newspaper today.\" [607] The girl smiled, a little nervous. \"Oh, so that's how it is!\" She said, \"I'm starting to get scared. Sir, what do you want with me?\" [608] \"Official business - always official business,\" Detective Quin said pitifully, \"I'm interested in anyone, I always relate it to my profession... before asking you questions, I want to ask, who are your friends?\" [609] When Detective Quin looked at the three of them, all three coughed awkwardly. Miss Francis smiled charmingly. \"I apologize, sir, please allow me to introduce my friends. These two ladies are Hilda Orangi and Eve Ellis, my good friends; this is Mr. Stephen Barry, my fianc\u00e9.\" [610] Detective Quin looked at them with a touch of surprise. \"If I'm not mistaken, you're all members of the cast of 'Gunfight', right?\" [611] All three nodded in unison. [612] Queene turned to Francis. \"I don't mean to pry, Miss Eves-Pope, but I want you to explain... why your friends are here with you?\" [613] The three actors stood up very awkwardly. Francis looked from her companions to Queene, pleading with her eyes. [614] \"I\u2014I'm sorry, officer,\" she said quickly. \"I... I've never been questioned by the police before. I'm nervous, and I want my fianc\u00e9 and these two ladies\u2014my closest friends\u2014to be here with me when you ask me questions. I didn't realize it went against your wishes...\u201d\n[615] \"I understand,\" said Queene, smiling. \"I completely understand. But, you see\u2014\" he made a gesture for them to go out first. [616] Stephen Barry leaned over the girl's chair, a challenging look in his eyes as he stared at Quinn. \"Darling, I won't leave if you need me.\" [617] \"But, Stephen, dear\u2014\" Frances helplessly sobbed. Quinn's expression remained firm, showing no signs of relenting. \"You...you should go out first. But please wait outside for me. It won't be long, right, sir?\" she asked, her eyes showing displeasure. [618] Quinn shook his head. \"Not too long.\" His attitude had changed completely, seeming to become somewhat irritable. The other three seemed to sense his change, subtly aligning themselves against him. [619] Hilda Orlandi was a tall, plump woman in her forties, still attractive despite her age, with traces of her youthful beauty on her face. Under the cold light in the room, her face lost its color. She leaned over Frances, staring at Detective Quinn with a firm tone in her voice. \"We'll wait for you outside, dear, if you feel uncomfortable or anything, just call out, and we'll come right in to help you.\" She huffed out of the room, followed by Eve Ellis, who patted Frances' hand comfortingly. \"Don't worry, Frances, we're all here for you.\" She tugged on Barry's arm and followed Hilda Orlandi out. Barry glanced back at Quinn, a mix of anger and concern in his eyes. He glared at Quinn again before slamming the door shut. [620] Quinn immediately stood up, his movements clean and his attitude cold. He stared into Frances' eyes, his hand resting on the surface of the table. \"Now, Frances Eves-Pope, Miss,\" he said succinctly, \"I'd like to talk to you about this...\u201d He reached into his pocket, pulling out a rhinestone purse like a stage magician. \"I'd like to return your purse to you.\" [621] Francis sat up a little, her gaze shifting from Detective Quinn to the sparkling handbag. Her face turned pale. \"Oh, that's... that's my evening bag!\" [622] \"Indeed, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn said, \"it was found at the theater\u2014tonight.\" [623] \"Of course!\" She smiled nervously and sat back down. \"I'm such a fool! I didn't even know my bag was missing until now...\"\n[624] \"But, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Detective Quinn continued solemnly, \"where we found your bag is more important than finding the bag itself.\" [625] Francis opened her mouth wide, staring at him with fear in her eyes. \"Yes, I heard about it.\" She whispered. [626] \"Well, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn coldly continued, \"your bag was found in the pocket of the deceased!\" [627] Fear filled the girl's eyes. She let out a sob and swayed on her chair, her face pale and tense. [628] Quinn leaped forward, a look of pity and concern on his face. He touched Francis's weak body just as the door burst open and Stephen Barry rushed into the room, his coat flapping behind him. Hilda Orlancy, Eve Ellis, and Johnson followed close behind. [629] \"What did you do to her, you damned detective!\" Barry shouted, shoving Quinn aside. He gently held Francis in his arms, brushing aside the strand of black hair that was in front of her face and whispering comforting words in her ear. Francis sighed and looked up at the agitated face next to her, confused. \"Stephen, I...I feel dizzy,\" she murmured, and then sank back into Stephen's embrace. [630] \"Bring some water!\" the young man exclaimed, stroking her hand. Johnson immediately handed over a flat glass cup. Barry forced a few gulps down her throat, and Francis coughed and regained her senses. The two actresses pushed Barry aside and sternly ushered the men out of the room. Quinn followed the protesting young man and the detective out the door with a submissive expression. [631] \"You're a good cop, aren't you?\" Barry sniped at Quinny. \"What did you do to her \u2013 give her a whack on the head with your usual police tactics?\" [632] \"Oh, oh, young man,\" Quinny said mildly, \"don't talk so harshly. She was just frightened.\" [633] They all stood there in silence, the tension palpable. The door opened, and the two actresses emerged, supporting Francis. Barry rushed to her side, stroking her hand and whispering, \"Do you feel better now, dear?\" [634] \"Stephen \u2013 take me \u2013 home,\" she gasped, leaning on Barry. [635] Quinny stepped aside, letting them pass. He watched them slowly make their way to the front door, accompanied by a small crowd of spectators. [636] Chapter Six: The District Attorney Becomes a Biographer\n[637] Detective Richard Quinn was a unique man. Although small in stature, he was remarkably sturdy; his head was full of white hair, and his face was etched with lines that spoke of his rich life experiences.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe could have been a corporate executive or a night watchman, depending on his whim. Of course, with the right clothes, his unremarkable build could pass for any profession. [638] This adaptability was also evident in his demeanor. No one could truly understand him. He was a source of wonder for his colleagues, his enemies, and those wretches he handed over to the legal system. He could be any kind of personality, from mild-mannered to arrogant, from paternal to obstinate, depending on his whim. [639] However, deep down, as some more emotional people would argue, Quinn had a \"heart of gold.\" He had no malice in his heart, was warm-hearted, and was not affected by the cruel world. For those who dealt with him for business or official matters, he was a chameleon, and indeed he was. He always showed different aspects of his personality, as he found it good: people never understood him, never knew what he was going to say or do, so they always had a bit of fear of him. [640] At this moment, he returned to Pensel's office alone, closed the door, paused the investigation, and returned to his true self. At this moment, his face was wrinkled - physically old, but mentally wise. The image of the frightened, terrified girl kept flashing in his mind. Seeing her frail, terrified face made him want to escape. Francis Eves-Pope embodied everything an aging person would want their daughter to have. Seeing her fianc\u00e9 desperately defending her made him feel ashamed. [641] He sighed, reached for his snuff box, and took a deep drag. Although he was usually thrifty, he was willing to spend money on this. [642] There was a sudden knock at the door, and he immediately changed into an officer's demeanor - sitting at the desk, pondering the case. In fact, he hoped that Ellery would come in. [643] He said warmly, \"Come in.\" The door opened, and a thin, bright-eyed man in a heavy coat and a wool scarf walked in. [644] \"Henry!\" the officer called, standing up. \"I thought the doctor told you to stay in bed!\" [645] District Attorney Henry Sampson winked at Quinn and sat down in an armchair. [646] \"Doctor,\" he lectured, \"Doctors are so annoying. Now, tell me about the case. How's it going?\" [647] He groaned, carefully feeling his throat. Detective Queen sat down. [648] \"For an adult, Henry,\" he said firmly, \"you're the most uncooperative patient I've ever seen. You'll get pneumonia if you're not careful.\" [649] \"Well,\" the district attorney grinned, \"I have insurance, so I should worry... Oh, you haven't answered my question yet.\" [650] \"Oh, yes,\" Queen grumbled, \"your question. The case, you ask? It's going nowhere, my dear Henry. Is that satisfactory for you?\" [651] \"Please be more detailed,\" Henry said, \"remember, I'm a patient, my head is still buzzing.\" [652] \"Henry,\" Quine said, leaning forward solemnly, \"I'll tell you, this case is the most difficult one we've ever encountered in our department...Your head is buzzing, isn't it? I don't even want to talk about my head.\" [653] Henry frowned, looking at Detective Quine. \"If it's really as you say\u2014I think\u2014this is not a good time. The election is coming soon, an unsolved murder case, if it falls into the hands of the opposition... \"\n[654] \"Well, that's just one aspect,\" Quine whispered, \"I haven't considered the case from the election perspective, Henry. Someone was killed\u2014to be honest, I have no idea who killed him or how he was killed.\" [655] \"I accept your kind criticism, officer,\" Henry's tone lightened, \"but if you knew what I heard just a few minutes ago\u2014on the phone...\"\n[656] \"Wait a moment, my dear Watson\u2014as Ellery often says,\" Quin chuckled\u2014his sudden change in demeanor was typical of him, \"I bet I know what happened. You were at home, probably in bed. The phone rang, and then you heard complaints, protests, grumbling, and various sounds a person makes when agitated. The voice said: 'I can't believe I'm being treated like a criminal, being detained by the police. I want Quin to be severely punished! He's a serious threat to personal freedom!' and so on, right?\" [657] \"My dear fellow!\" Sampson smiled. [658] \"This gentleman, the one who complained to you,\u201d the officer continued, \u201cis a short, chubby man with gold-rimmed glasses, speaks like a woman, and has a very unpleasant voice. He also showed great concern for 'a very good friend, District Attorney Henry,' which is touching, isn't it?\" [659] Sampson sat there, staring at Quin. Then, a smile spread across his eager face. [660] \"It's shocking, my dear Sherlock!\" he whispered, \"Since you know so much about my friend, perhaps you can easily tell me his name?\" [661] \"Ah - is it this guy, right?\" Quine said, blushing, \"I - Ellery, my child! Nice to see you.\" [662] Ellery entered the room, shaking Sampson's hand warmly. Sampson greeted him enthusiastically, having worked with him for a long time. Ellery inquired about the District Attorney's condition and then set a large jar of coffee and a paper bag on the table, which could not help but make one think of French pastries inside. [663] \"Come on, gentlemen, the big search is finally over, done, even though the yield was small, the hardworking investigators now deserve a nightcap,\" he chuckled, patting his father's shoulder affectionately. [664] \"But, Ellery!\" Quine exclaimed happily, \"this is a great surprise! Henry, do you want to join us and celebrate a little?\" [665] \"I don't know what you're celebrating, but count me in,\" Sampson said. The three then enjoyed their meal together. [666] \"How's it going over there, Ellery?\" the old man asked, sipping his coffee contentedly. [667] \"Gods can go without eating or drinking,\" Ellery mumbled while eating a cream puff, \"I'm not all-powerful. So, tell me first what happened in your temporary interrogation room... but I can tell you something you don't know. Mr. Libby - the owner of the ice cream shop - confirmed what Jesse Lynch said about ginger ale, and Miss Eleanor Libby confirmed everything that happened in the alley.\" [668] Quinn wiped his mouth with a large handkerchief and said, \"Well, whatever, let Plautti check the ginger ale to make sure everything is fine. As for me, I just asked a few people, and now there's nothing else to do.\" [669] \"Thanks,\" Ellery said calmly, \"your statement is perfect! Have you told the district attorney about the events of this chaotic night?\" [670] \"Gentlemen,\" Sampson said, putting down his cup, \"I'll tell you what I've learned. About half an hour ago, a very good friend of mine called me - he happens to have some background - and clearly told me that someone was killed during tonight's performance. He accused you of violating the law, saying that Detective Richard Quinn rushed into the theater like a whirlwind, followed by the little whirlwinds, and made everyone wait for an hour - completely out of order, absolutely unforgivable. He also testified that Detective Quinn accused him of a crime and even ordered the overbearing police to search him, his wife, and daughter before they left the theater. [671] \"That's all the information my informant provided \u2013 the rest was just insults and complaints, not relevant to the case. The other thing is that Mr. Wiley just told me the identity of the victim. And, gentlemen, that's the most interesting part of the whole incident.\" [672] \"You know as much about this case as I do, Ellery,\" Quine grumbled, \"if not more, because I think you're quite familiar with Mr. Field's business activities... Ellery, what happened during the search outside?\" [673] Ellery comfortably crossed his legs. \"You probably guessed it, too, that the search of the audience didn't yield any results. We didn't find anything out of the ordinary. No one looked suspicious, and no one confessed. So, the search was a failure.\" [674] \"Of course, of course,\" Quine said, \"the person who planned this murder was very clever. I suppose you didn't see any suspicious extra hats?\" [675] \"This, Father,\" Ellery said, \"is why I'm here in the hall. No, there were no suspicious hats.\" [676] \"Is everything over over there?\" [677] \"I just finished buying pastries on the street,\" Ellery said, \"there was nothing going on over there either. They just had to make the angry audience line up and go out. Now, everyone's out, including the audience upstairs, the theater staff, and the cast\u2026 These actors are quite peculiar, they spend the whole night pretending to be God, pretending to be all-powerful; then suddenly they find themselves reduced to mere mortals, subject to the same troubles and diseases as everyone else. By the way, Willie searched the five people who left the office. The young lady has a very nice car. I guess it's Miss Evans-Pope and her friends\u2026 I don't know, but you probably forgot about them by now.\" [678] \"So, we're out of ideas, are we?\" Quine grumbled, \"The case is this.\" [679] \"That's it,\" Quine summarized after succinctly recounting what had happened in the small office, \"Now, Henry, you must have something to tell us about Monte Field. We know he's a very slick guy \u2014 but that's all we know about him.\" [680] \"You're being polite,\" Henry said harshly, \"I can recite his life story from memory. It seems you're in trouble now, and I think his past experiences might provide you with some clues.\" [681] \"Field was first investigated by our department during my predecessor's tenure,\" Henry continued. \"We suspected him of being involved in a scam involving an illegal syndicate. Assistant District Attorney Crowning was in charge of the case, but he couldn't catch Field on anything. Field's actions were very discreet. All we knew was what a snitch kicked out of the mob told us, but we couldn't verify the truth of it. Of course, Crowning never let Field know we suspected him. The case eventually cooled down, although Crowning persisted in his investigation, only to find nothing every time he thought he had something. Oh, you're right - Field is indeed cunning. [682] \"After I took office, on Crowning's strong recommendation, we conducted a detailed investigation into Field's background. Of course, it was done secretly. Our findings were that Monte Field was born into a New England aristocratic family, one that doesn't boast of being descendants of the Mayflower. He had private tutors as a child, then attended a prestigious private preparatory school, barely graduating. His father, clinging to a glimmer of hope, sent him to Harvard. Field seemed to be a bad egg from a young age. Although he didn't do anything illegal, his behavior was erratic. On the other hand, he had a strong sense of self-worth, so he shortened his last name to Field after a big argument with his father. [683] Quincannon and Ellery nodded. Ellery's eyes reflected with introspection, while Quincannon stared intently at Henry. [684] \"Field,\" Henry continued, \"is not entirely useless. On the contrary, he is very clever. He studied law at Harvard and excelled. He seems to have a talent for rhetoric, of course, thanks to his extensive legal knowledge. But just after graduation, before his family could enjoy any of the fruits of his education, he got involved in a dirty affair with a girl. His father immediately disinherited him. Then he was kicked out - he brought shame to the family - you know...\n[685] \"However, our friend was not defeated by misfortune. He used the little money he had left to venture out and make his own way. However, we don't know how he managed to survive during that time. Next, we learned that he had formed a partnership with a man named Cohen. Cohen was one of the most crooked lawyers around. What a partnership it was! They picked the biggest con artists in the gang and defended them. Through this, they made a lot of money. Now, you should understand how difficult it is to catch someone like that. They knew the loopholes in the law better than even the justices of the Supreme Court. They were not punished - it was a golden age for crime. The con artists felt they were a cut above the rest, as long as Cohen and Field defended them.\" [686] \"Among them, Cohen was experienced and knowledgeable, handling the 'contact' with the company's clients and determining the fees \u2013 despite his imperfect English, he did an excellent job \u2013 oh, and Cohen's fate was also tragic. He was murdered one winter evening, shot in the head. Although it has been twelve years, the killer has not been found. That is \u2013 from a legal standpoint, he has not been found. We have long suspected his identity. With Field's death tonight, I am not surprised that Cohen's case can be dropped. [687] \"So, he was just a playboy,\" Ellery murmured. \"Even after death, his face looks repulsive. I'm sorry I didn't buy the first edition of that novel.\" [688] \"Forget it, bookworm,\" Quin growled. \"Henry, continue.\" [689] \"Now \u2013\" Sampson picked up the last piece of cake from the table and savored it, \"now, let's talk about the good side of Field's life. After his partner's unfortunate death, he seemed to want to change and start anew. He started working \u2013 real legal work \u2013 and he was smart, always able to get things done. For several years, he worked alone, slowly erasing the stain on his reputation in the legal field, even occasionally gaining the respect of some of the proud bigwigs in the field. [690] \"This good performance \u2013 at least on the surface \u2013 lasted for six years. Then, he met Benjamin Morgan \u2013 a reliable, unblemished man with a good reputation, but lacking the talent to become a great lawyer. For some reason, Field convinced Morgan to work with him. After that, things got interesting.\" [691] \"You should remember that there were many illegal things happening in New York at that time, right? We vaguely felt that there was a huge criminal gang consisting of fence, con artists, and lawyers, with some politicians involved in some cases. There were several major robbery cases; bootlegging was rampant in the suburbs. Many people openly robbed and even killed, keeping the police busy. But we all knew that although you caught some of them, you didn't destroy the gang and didn't catch their leader. I have every reason to believe that our late friend, Monte Field, was the mastermind behind the whole thing. [692] \"With such a clever man, doing such things was a piece of cake. With Cohen's guidance, Monte Field had become very familiar with the brains of the entire underworld. After Cohen lost his utility value, he was killed offhand. Then, Field - remember, I'm mainly guessing here, because there's almost no evidence - then, Field openly began to engage in respectable legal affairs while quietly establishing a widespread criminal organization. Of course, we don't know how he did that. When everything was ready and prepared to do something big, he first teamed up with the respected and renowned Morgan, ensuring his legal status, and then began to plan the numerous major fraud cases of the past five years ...\n[693] \"What role did Morgan play in this?\" Ellery asked casually. [694] \"I was just about to talk about Morgan. We have every reason to believe that Morgan is absolutely innocent and has no connection with Field's secret criminal activities. He is a straight man, in fact, he often refuses to defend those with a bad reputation. When Morgan realized what was going on, their relationship must have been very tense. I don't know if that's the case - you can easily get an answer from Morgan. Anyway, they eventually went their separate ways. Since their partnership was dissolved, Field became more and more open, but we still had no concrete evidence and could not bring him to trial. [695] \"Sorry, I interrupt, Henry,\" Quin said thoughtfully, \"Can you provide more information about their breakup? Next time when questioning Morgan, I can also use it to verify what he says.\" [696] \"Of course, I can!\" Henry replied firmly. \"I'm glad you reminded me. Before they finally decided to part ways, they had a big argument that almost came to blows. They were having lunch at the Webster Club when they started arguing fiercely. The argument escalated, and eventually, someone had to intervene. Morgan was furious and even threatened to kill Field. I think Field must have been very calm at that time.\" [697] \"Did anyone witness why they argued?\" Quinny asked. [698] \"Unfortunately, no one knows. The situation quickly calmed down, and they parted ways peacefully. Since then, no one has heard anything about them.\" [699] After the district attorney finished speaking, the room fell into a contemplative silence. Ellery played a few notes of Schubert's music, and Quinny took a small pinch of snuff. [700] \"I would say, although I'm not entirely sure,\" Ellery said, looking up at the ceiling, \"that Morgan is now in big trouble.\" [701] His father muttered. Sampson said solemnly, \"Well, that's your business, gentlemen. I know what to do. Since Field is no longer around, I'll go through his files and records in detail. The rest doesn't matter. I just hope that after Field's murder, his whole gang will come crashing down. I'll send someone to his office tomorrow morning.\" [702] \"One of my men is already there,\" Quin said casually. \"So, you think it's Morgan, right?\" He asked, glancing at Ellery. [703] \"I seem to have already said it,\" Ellery said calmly. \"Basically, Morgan is in trouble now. There's nothing else. I admit, logically speaking, Morgan seems like the killer \u2013 except for one thing, gentlemen.\" [704] \"The hat,\" Quin said quickly. [705] \"No,\" Ellery said. \"Another hat.\" [706] Chapter 7: The Quinns Analyze the Case\n[707] \"Let's see where we stand now,\" Ellery continued. \"From the most basic angle, the facts are as follows: Monte Field was a notorious figure before his death, likely the head of a large criminal organization, and undoubtedly had many enemies. He was murdered at around 9:55 pm, just before the end of the second act at the Roman Theater. The person who discovered the case was named William Pusak, an employee with low intelligence. He was sitting in the same row as Field, five seats apart.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nI'm here as quickly as I can. Where's the victim?\" [263] Detective Quin pointed to the corpse on the ground, and Plautti immediately knelt by the corpse on the pavement, starting to work. A police officer was called over to provide a flashlight for the assistant medical examiner. [264] Detective Quin pulled Jesse's arm to the side and asked, \"Jesse, what happened after he asked you to buy ginger ale?\" [265] The boy watched the proceedings, took a deep breath when the officer asked, and continued, \"Well, sir, I told him we don't sell ginger ale, only orange water. He leaned forward slightly, and I could smell the alcohol on his breath. Then, he whispered to me, 'If you go and get me a bottle, I'll give you fifty cents, kid. But I need it right away.' You know, no one gives tips anymore...well, I told him I couldn't go right away, but I could slip out and get it for him when the second act started. He agreed, gave me his seat number, and walked away. I watched him go back into the theater. After the intermission ended, the usher closed the door, and I left the alleyway stall, crossed the street, and ran to Libby's ice cream shop. I -\"\n[266] \"Jesse, do you often leave your stall?\" [267] \"No, sir. I usually bring everything with me and run inside the theater before the usher closes the door, then move my stall to the downstairs lounge. But the man said he needed the ginger ale right away, and I thought I could get it for him first, which would be quicker. Then I'd come back to the aisle, grab my stuff, and go in through the front door. Nobody would say anything...well, I left my stall, ran to Lippy's, bought a bottle of ginger ale, and sneaked back into the theater to find the man. He gave me a dollar. I thought he was a nice guy, because he said he'd only give me fifty cents.\" [268] \"Jesse, you've done well,\" Inspector Queen said approvingly. \"Now, just a few more questions. Was he sitting in this seat? Did he ask you to come here?\" [269] \"Yes, sir. He said it was LL32 left, and I found him right here.\" [270] \"Good.\u201d Inspector Queen paused for a moment before asking casually, \"Jesse, did you notice if he was alone?\" [271] \"Yes, sir. He was alone. I noticed that because the theater is usually full since it opened, and I found it strange that he had so many empty seats around him.\" [272] \"Jesse, you did well. You would make a good detective... I think you don't remember how many empty seats there were?\" [273] \"Well, sir, it was quite dark in the theater, and I didn't pay much attention. I think there were probably six in total, a few in his row, next to him, and a few in the front, right in front of him.\" [274] \"Jesse, wait a moment.\" [275] The boy turned around, licking his lips, and was clearly a bit afraid when he heard Ellery's low, cold voice. [276] \"Did you see his fancy hat again when you served him the ginger ale, Jesse?\" Ellery asked, tapping the tip of his cane on his clean shoe. [277] \"Ah, yes... yes, sir.\" The boy stammered, \"His hat was on his knees when I served him the ginger ale, but before I left, I saw him put it under the seat.\" [278] \"One more question, Jesse.\" The boy breathed a sigh of relief at the sound of the officer's encouraging voice. \"Do you think it took you about ten minutes to get the drink to him after the second act started?\" [279] Jesse Lynch thought carefully before answering confidently, \"About ten minutes, sir. We had to keep track of time. I said ten minutes because when I came in with the bottle, the stage was at the part where the girl was captured in the gang's hideout and being interrogated by the thugs.\" [280] \"You're a sharp-eyed Hermes, young man!\" Ellery exclaimed softly, suddenly smiling. Jesse's last trace of fear disappeared as he saw the smile. He smiled back at Ellery. Ellery leaned in and asked, \"Now, tell me, boy, how could it have taken you ten minutes to buy a bottle of ginger ale, walk back to the theater, and return? That's quite a long time, isn't it?\" [281] Jesse's face turned red, and he looked from Ellery to Quin, blushing. \"Uh, sir... I think it's because I stopped for a while and chatted with my girlfriend.\" [282] \"Your girlfriend?\" Quin asked curiously. [283] \"Yes, sir. Her name is Eleanor Lively, and her father owns the ice cream shop. When I went to buy the ginger beer, she... she wanted me to stay and hang out with her.\" [284] \"The ice cream shop's alleyway stall?\" Ellery said eagerly. \"Right, Jesse, your stall is in the alleyway. You're not telling me you went back to the alleyway just because you felt like it.\" [285] \"Of course we went back!\" the boy answered, surprised by Ellery's reaction. \"I mean, both of us went back, me and Eleanor.\" [286] \"You and Eleanor! Ah, Jesse,\" Ellery said calmly. \"How long were you there?\" [287] Detective Queene's eyes gleamed with interest as he murmured approvingly and listened attentively to the boy's answer. [288] \"Well, I wanted to move my booth away as soon as possible, sir. But Eleanor and I... we wanted to chat, so Eleanor suggested that we could stay in the alley until the next intermission. I thought it was a good idea, so I decided to wait until the act was almost over, around ten minutes to ten, and then sneak in to get some orange juice. When the usher opened the door for the second intermission, I'd be ready. So, we left the booth there, sir... that's not wrong, is it, sir? I didn't want to do anything bad.\" [289] Ellery sat up straight, looking directly into the boy's eyes. \"Jesse, I hope you'll answer me carefully: What exact time did you and Eleanor go back to the alley?\" [290] \"Uh...,\" Jesse scratched his head. \"I gave the ginger ale to that person around nine twenty-five. Then I went to find Eleanor, stayed with her for a while, and then went back to the alley. I got back to my booth... uh... probably around nine thirty-five.\" [291] \"Good. What time did you leave the alley exactly?\" [292] \"Ten o'clock, sir. I asked Eleanor if I should go get the orange juice, and she looked at her watch, and it was ten o'clock.\" [293] \"Did you hear anything about what happened at the theater?\" [294] \"No, sir. I think we were just chatting... I had no idea anything happened at the theater. Later, I left the alley and ran into Johnny Tice, the usher, who was standing there like a guard. He told me that something happened at the theater, and Mr. Pensel ordered him to stand outside the left alley. That's when I found out.\" [295] \"I see...,\" Ellery said, somewhat excitedly as he took off his glasses and waved them in front of the boy's nose, \"Jesse, think carefully now, was anyone going in or out of the alley while you and Eleanor were there?\" [296] The boy answered promptly and confidently, \"No, sir, not a single person.\" [297] \"Alright, young man.\" Detective Quin gently patted the boy's back, and the boy smiled and left at Quin's prompting. Quin looked around sharply and observed Mr. Pencroft closely, noticing that his announcement on stage didn't seem to have any effect. So, Quin beckoned him over and said, \"Mr. Pencroft, I'd like to know the schedule of the play...what time does Act Two start?\" [298] \"Act Two starts officially at 9:15 and ends at 10:05.\" Mr. Pencroft answered promptly. [299] \"So, was the performance tonight scheduled according to that?\" [300]\n[301] \"Of course,\" the manager said. \"Due to cues, lighting, and other factors, we must be on time.\" [302] Officer Quinn calculated silently in his mind, \"So, at 9:25, the boy saw Field alive.\" [303] Officer Quinn suddenly turned around and called out to Detective Dahl. Detective Dahl ran over. [304] \"Detective Dahl,\" the officer asked, \"Do you remember when Pazak told you the exact time he found the murder?\" [305] Detective Dahl scratched his head. \"I can't remember, sir,\" he said. \"I just remember that it was almost the end of Act Two when it happened.\" [306] \"That's too vague, Detective Dohr,\" said the officer impatiently. \"Where are the actors?\" [307] \"We have them gathered over there, just behind the foyer, sir,\" answered Detective Dohr. \"We don't know what else to do with them.\" [308] \"Bring one over,\" the officer ordered sharply. [309] Detective Dohr ran off. Quien Officer beckoned to Piggott, who was only a few feet away, standing between a man and a woman. [310] \"Bring the doorman over, Piggott,\" ordered Quien Officer. Piggott nodded and brought over a waddling, overweight tall man in a uniform whose clothes clung tightly to his sagging muscles, and whose hat trembled in his hand. [311] \"Are you the doorman standing outside the theater?\" the officer asked. [312] \"Yes, sir,\" the doorman answered, nervously fidgeting with his hat. [313] \"Good. Now, think carefully, has anyone - I mean anyone - left the theater during the second act?\" [314] The old man thought for a moment and answered firmly, \"No, sir, no one has left the theater. I mean, except for the boy selling orange juice, no one has left.\" [315] \"Were you there the whole time?\" Quine officer asked loudly. [316] \"Yes, sir.\" [317] \"So, do you remember anyone going in during the second act?\" [318] \"Well, uh... Jesse Lynch, the boy selling orange juice, went in at the beginning of the second act.\" [319] \"What about the others?\" [320] The old man did not speak, but instead tried to think hard. After a while, he looked from one face to another, his eyes filled with despair. Then, he murmured, \"I don't remember, sir.\" [321] Inspector Quin looked at him impatiently. The old man seemed tense and sincere. He was sweating profusely and occasionally glanced at Pendergast, as if he suspected that his poor memory might cost him his job. [322] \"I'm really sorry, sir,\" the porter said again. \"Really sorry. It seems someone did come in, but my memory isn't what it used to be. I... I can't seem to remember.\" [323] Ellery interrupted the old man's heavy-accented speech with a calm tone. [324] \"How long have you been the doorkeeper?\" [325] The old man turned his confused gaze to the new interrogator. \"About ten years, sir. I wasn't a doorkeeper before, but as I got older, I couldn't do much else...\"\n[326] \"I understand,\" Ellery said gently. He hesitated for a moment before firmly saying, \"As a doorkeeper for so many years, you might have forgotten if someone came in during the first act. But usually, very few people come in during the second act. So, no matter what, you should be able to remember if you think hard about it.\" [327] The old man replied with a pained expression, \"I... I really can't remember, sir. I could say no one came in, but that wouldn't be true. But I just can't answer.\" [328] \"It's okay,\" Detective Quinn said, placing his hand on the old man's shoulder. \"Never mind, we may have asked too much. Let's stop here for now.\" The poor old man shuffled away. [329] At this point, Dr. Dohle walked towards the crowd, followed by a tall, handsome man wearing a tweed coat and still carrying traces of stage makeup on his face. [330] \"This is Mr. Peel, the lead actor in the play,\" Dr. Dohle reported. [331] Detective Quean smiled at the actor and extended his hand. \"Nice to meet you, Mr. Peel. Perhaps you can provide me with a clue.\" [332] \"It's my pleasure to assist you, officer,\" Peel replied in a deep voice. He glanced at the back of the pathologist busy with the body and then looked away with a look of disgust. [333] \"I suppose you were on stage when the audience started shouting, right?\" Detective Quean asked. [334] \"Yes, in fact, everyone from the cast was there. What do you want to know?\" [335] \"Can you tell me the exact time when you noticed the commotion in the audience?\" [336] \"Sure. There were about ten minutes left in the show. It was the climax of the play, and my character was supposed to shoot. We had discussed this point during rehearsals, so we were pretty sure of the time.\" [337] Quinn nodded. \"Thank you, Mr. Pierce, that's exactly what I wanted to know... by the way, I apologize for having you all crammed back here. We've been very busy and couldn't make any other arrangements, so please bear with us. You and the rest of the cast can now go backstage. Of course, please don't leave until you're notified.\" [338] \"I understand, officer. It's nice to be able to help,\" Pierce bowed and returned to the back of the theater. [339] Quinn leaned against the nearest chair and fell into thought. Ellery stood beside him, absent-mindedly wiping his glasses. [340] \"Well, Ellery?\" Quinn asked quietly. [341] \"Just preliminary information, my dear Watson.\" Watson mutters, \"Someone saw our 'esteemed' victim alive at 9:25, and then found him dead at 9:55. The question is: what happened in between? It sounds simple enough, but...\"\n[342] \"Not necessarily.\" Detective Quin grumbles, \"Pigott!\" [343] \"Yes, sir.\" [344] \"This is the usher, isn't it? Have her come over here.\" [345] Pigott releases the arm of the young woman standing beside him. She is elegantly dressed, heavily made up, with a dazzling smile that seems almost sinister. She saunters over, brazenly inspecting Detective Quin. [346] \"Are you the fixed usher in this aisle, Miss? What's your name?\" Detective Quinn asked in a casual tone. [347] \"Yes, I am. My name is O'Connell, Maggie O'Connell.\" [348] Detective Quinn gently took her arm, saying, \"I'm afraid you'll have to be brave, dear. Come over here for a moment.\" When they stopped at LL row, the girl's face turned pale. \"Sorry to interrupt, Dr. Plautti, hope you don't mind.\" Detective Quinn said. [349] Dr. Plautti looked up, frowning. \"Not at all, officer. I'll be done shortly.\" He stood up, holding a cigar, and moved to the side. [350] The girl leaned over the body, and Detective Quinn closely observed her expression. She took a deep breath. [351] \"Do you remember bringing this person to his seat, Miss O'Connell?\" [352] The girl hesitated, then said, \"I think I remember this person, but, as usual, I've been very busy tonight, giving seats to almost two hundred people, so I'm not entirely sure.\" [353] \"Do you remember these empty seats \u2014\" Inspector Quinn pointed to the seven empty seats, \"\u2014 were empty during the first act?\" [354] \"Uh... it seemed like this way when I walked up and down the aisle... yes, no one was sitting here. I remember no one sitting there all night.\" [355] \"Did anyone walk up and down the aisle during the second act, Miss O'Connell? Try to remember, your answer is crucial.\" [356] The girl hesitated again, casting a quick glance at the stern officer before saying, \"No... I didn't see anyone walking up and down the aisle. I can't give you much to go on, I know nothing about this. I'm just a hardworking girl, I -\"\n[357] \"Yes, yes, dear, we understand. Where do you usually stand when you're not seating people?\" [358] The girl pointed to the front of the aisle. [359] \"Were you in that spot during the second act, Miss O'Connell?\" Sergeant Queen asked with a smile. [360] The girl licked her lips and said, \"Well, yes, I was. But honestly, I didn't notice anything out of the ordinary all night.\" [361] \"Very well,\" the detective said in a calm tone, \"that's all for now.\" The girl turned and walked away briskly. [362] There was a small commotion in the crowd. The detective approached Plautti, who stood up, closing his box with a somewhat gloomy whistle. [363] \"Well, doctor, I assume you're done with the examination. What's the verdict?\" the detective asked. [364] \"The conclusion is quite simple, sir. He died two hours ago. At first, I was puzzled by the cause of death, but now I'm sure it was poison. The signs indicate that he died from some kind of alcohol poisoning, which you may have noticed from his yellowish skin. And you probably smelled the alcohol in his mouth, which was the most fragrant I've ever smelled. He was certainly drunk out of his mind. However, this was not just a regular case of alcohol poisoning, otherwise he wouldn't have died so quickly. That's all I can tell you for now.\" [365] The detective took out a bottle wrapped in a handkerchief from his pocket and handed it to Dr. Plautti, saying, \"This is the deceased's, doctor. I hope you can help me analyze the contents. Before that, let Jimmy take it to the lab for a detailed check for fingerprints. Oh, and - wait a moment.\" The detective looked around, picked up the half-empty bottle of ginger ale that had fallen on the carpet corner, \"Please analyze this ginger ale for me, doctor.\" He added. [366] The assistant coroner put the bottle of liquor and the bottle of ginger ale in his bag and adjusted his hat lightly. [367] \"Well, sir, I'm leaving. I'll give you a more detailed report after the autopsy, which should help with your investigation.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nPusak stood up to squeeze past Field, and at that moment, Field was already poisoned. Pusak heard him murmur, \"Murder! Murdered!\" or something similar. [708] \"Pusak called a policeman, who then brought a doctor from the audience to check if Field was dead. The doctor confirmed that the victim had died from some kind of alcohol poisoning. Later, Assistant Coroner Plauti confirmed this, but he also added that there was an interference factor - usually, even if someone dies from alcohol poisoning, it won't happen so quickly. So, we can put aside the specific cause of death for now, as it can only be determined after autopsy. [709] \"Since there were many people in the audience and order needed to be maintained, the policeman called for help. The nearby police arrived to assist. They immediately launched an investigation. The first important question was whether the killer had the opportunity to leave the crime scene after the murder. Detective Doyle, who was the first to arrive at the crime scene, immediately ordered the theater manager to place people at every exit and both sides of the corridors to guard them. [710] \"When I arrived here, I immediately thought of this point and conducted a simple investigation myself. I circled all the exits and asked the people guarding them. I found that there was someone guarding every door in the theater during the first and second acts, of course, there were two exceptions that I will mention later. Now, through the boy who sells orange juice, Jesse Lynch, we can confirm that the victim was still alive not only during the intermission between the first and second acts - Lynch had seen him in the corridor and talked to him - but also about ten minutes after the second act began, when Lynch had delivered ginger ale to his seat, where he was later found dead. There is a usher standing under the stairs leading to the second floor in the theater, who swears that no one went up or down during the second act. This also rules out the possibility of the killer going upstairs. [711] \"The two exceptions I mentioned are the two doors at the leftmost aisle, which should have been guarded but were not, because the usher Maggie O'Connell was sitting next to her sweetheart at the time. This made me think of a possibility that the killer might have left through one of these two doors. If the killer was so talented, these two doors would be very convenient for escape. But the usher's testimony ruled out this possibility. This was something I discovered after my father asked her, and I followed up with more questions. [712] \"You snuck up on her, you little brat?\" Quinns glared at Ellery. \"I did talk to her,\" Ellery said with a smile. \"And I found an important fact that is closely related to our current investigation. O'Connell swore that she locked the door inside and out with the floor lock before leaving the two doors and sitting next to Johnny Parsen. When the commotion started, she immediately stood up from the 'priest' and ran to the door, only to find it still locked, just like when she left. So, she opened the door, and at that moment, Detective Dohal was trying to get the audience to quiet down. Unless she lied - but I don't think so. This also proves that the killer did not leave through these two doors, as the door was still locked from the inside when the body was discovered.\" [713] \"Well, I guess I'll have to change careers!\" Quinns grumbled. \"She didn't tell me anything about this, you damn woman! I'll deal with her later, this hateful woman!\" [714] \"Please be rational, Mr. Peacekeeper,\" Ellery said with a smile. \"She didn't tell you about locking the door because you didn't ask her. And she probably felt bad enough already.\" [715] \"The floor lock was a critical piece of evidence that helped us to narrow down the list of suspects to the two ushers, Maggie O'Connell and Jesse Lynch. However, the fact that the\n[716] \"Anyway, through her story, we can rule out the possibility that the killer escaped through the doors near the victim. I admit there are many possibilities - for example, Maggie O'Connell could be an accomplice. I'm just suggesting this as a possibility, not an opinion. At least in my opinion, the killer wouldn't take the risk of being seen leaving through those side doors. Moreover, leaving in an unusual way at an unusual time would be highly noticeable, especially during the second act when almost no one left. Besides, the killer couldn't have known that O'Connell would be neglecting her duties - unless she was an accomplice. Since this case was carefully planned - we must admit, the evidence suggests so - the killer might have given up the idea of escaping through the side doors.\" [717] \"The escape route, I think, is only one left, and that is the main entrance. However, the ticket taker and the doorkeeper outside confirmed that no one left the theater during the second act. Of course, except for the boy selling oranges, who was irrelevant.\" [718] \"All the exits were either guarded or locked, and from 9:35, there was someone in the alley - first Linnie, then Eleanor, then the male usher - Johnny Thurston - and then the police - these facts, along with my questioning and investigation, gentlemen. The inevitable conclusion is that the killer has been in the theater since the body was found!\" [719] After Ellery finished speaking, the room fell silent. \"By the way,\" Ellery added calmly, \"I remember asking the ushers if they saw anyone leave their seats during the second act, and they all said they couldn't remember anyone changing seats.\" [720] Quin absent-mindedly took a pinch of snuff. \"Well done - a reasonable deduction, my son. But ultimately, it's nothing surprising or decisive. Even if the killer was still in the theater, how can we catch him?\" [721] \"He didn't say you could catch him,\" said Sampson, smiling. \"Don't be so sensitive, old friend; nobody will say you were derelict in your duty during the investigation. As far as I know, you handled the whole thing very well tonight.\" [722] Quinn muttered, \"I admit, my not having thoroughly questioned the matter of the door bothers me a bit. But even if the murderer might have left immediately after the crime, I would still have asked the same question, hoping he would still be in the theater.\" [723] \"But, father\u2014of course!\" Ellery said solemnly, \"You have so many things to attend to, and my task is simply to sit back and observe, like Socrates.\" [724] \"So far, how have the people who have been surveyed been doing?\" Sampson curiously asked. [725] \"Hmm, how are they?\" Eleanor questioned, \"We can't seem to get a clear answer from either talking to them or observing their actions. We caught Parsons Johnny, the villain, but it's clear he was just a fan of the show, looking for some juicy gossip about his colleagues. As for Maggie O'Connell, she's suspicious, but we can't make any conclusions at this stage. She could be an accomplice, innocent, or just careless \u2013 she could play any role. Then there's William Pusack, who discovered Field. But have you noticed how slow-witted he is? And Benjamin Morgan \u2013 he's where we found the potential lead. But what can we make of his behavior tonight? Yes, the letter and the free ticket do sound odd, as anyone could have written that letter, even Morgan himself. I must also remember his public threat to kill Field; and the animosity between them, although the reason is unclear, has been going on for two years. Finally, there's Miss Frances Eves-Pope. Unfortunately, I wasn't present when you spoke with her. However, the fact remains that we found her handbag in the deceased's pocket \u2013 isn't that interesting? Could you explain that, please?\" [726] \"So far, that's the situation,\" Ellery continued regretfully. \"We have too many doubts from tonight's case, but too few facts.\" [727] \"So far, son,\" said Quin calmly. \"Your reasoning is flawless. But you've forgotten one important thing\u2014the empty seats. And another surprising fact\u2014the ticket stub of Field, and the only one that could possibly belong to the killer\u2014I mean the LL30 left one that Flint found\u2014these two tickets don't match. That is, the tear marks on the tickets show that the ticket taker checked these two tickets at different times!\" [728] \"Right,\" said Ellery. \"But let's put that aside for now and talk about Field's hat.\" [729] \"That hat\u2014uh, what do you think?\" Quin asked curiously. [730] \"Well, first, we're quite sure the hat didn't disappear by accident. Jesse Lynch saw the dead man's hat on his knees about ten minutes after the second act started. But now the hat is gone, and the only logical explanation is that the killer took it. Now\u2014no matter where the hat is\u2014we can immediately draw one of two conclusions: either the hat itself is connected to the case, and leaving it behind might reveal the killer's identity. We don't know what the connection is yet; or, the hat may contain something the killer wants. You might ask, why didn't the killer take what was inside and leave the hat behind? If this assumption is correct, it may be because he didn't have enough time to take it out, or he didn't know how to take it out, so he took the hat to study it later. Agree with my reasoning so far?\" [731] The district attorney nodded slowly. Quincannon sat motionless, a look of confusion in his eyes. [732] \"Now let's think about what might be in the hat. Ellery continued, wiping his glasses vigorously. \"Based on the size, shape, and volume of the hat, our range of possibilities is not very broad. What could be in a hat? Some of the things I can think of are mainly: some document, jewelry, money, or any other valuable small item that wouldn't be easily found in the hat. Obviously, this mysterious thing couldn't be in the top of the hat, because as soon as he took it off, it would fall out. So, we can conclude that whatever it is, it should be inside the hat. This immediately narrows down the possibilities. Solid large items can be ruled out. It could be jewelry, money, or documents. I think, through our understanding of Flint, we can rule out jewelry. If he carried something important, it would probably be related to his profession. [733] \"In the preliminary analysis of the missing hat, there is one more thing we need to consider. And, gentlemen, this should be crucial to our case before we solve it, that is, whether the killer knew before the murder that he had to take the hat away. In other words, did the killer know the importance of the hat, no matter how important it was? In my opinion, whether it's from the facts or from logical reasoning, it can be shown that the killer did not know the importance of the hat beforehand. [734] \"Please follow my line of thinking... Since Flint's hat is missing and no other hat was found in his place, it's clear that the hat was taken, and that's a fact. You should agree, as I pointed out earlier, that the killer is the most likely person to take the hat. Good! Let's put aside why he took the hat for now, we still have two choices: one, the killer knew beforehand that he had to take the hat; two, he didn't know beforehand that he had to take the hat. Let's examine the first possibility. If he knew beforehand, we can logically assume that the killer should have brought another hat to the theater to replace Flint's hat, instead of taking the victim's hat directly, leaving a clear clue. Bringing a replacement hat would be safer. And it shouldn't be difficult for the killer to find such a hat, because if he knew its importance beforehand, he would have further investigated the dimensions, style, and other details of Flint's hat. But we don't find a replacement hat here. We have every reason to believe that such a well-planned case should have had a replacement hat. But it's not there, so our only conclusion is that the killer didn't know the importance of the hat beforehand; otherwise, he would have taken preventive measures and left a hat. That way, the police would never know the importance of Flint's hat. [735] \"One more thing to confirm is that even if the killer had some unknown reason not to leave a replacement hat, he would have cut the hat open and\n[736] The district attorney stared at Ellery, his lips pressed together. Detective Quinn appeared to be dozing, his hand hovering between his snuff box and his nose. [737] \"What do you mean, Ellery?\" Sampson asked. \"Why is it so important that the killer didn't know the significance of the hat?\" [738] Ellery smiled. \"The murder occurred during the second act, and I wanted to use that to establish that if the killer didn't know the significance of the hat beforehand, he couldn't have planned the murder during the first intermission... Of course, Fielding's hat could appear somewhere in the theater, and once found, all these theories would be invalid. But... I don't think it will... \"\n[739] \"You've done some basic analysis, but it sounds logical,\" Sampson commended. \"You should become a lawyer.\" [740] \"You're no match for the Quinn family in terms of intellect,\" the old man suddenly broke into a grin. \"But I have to attend to something else related to the hat mystery. Ellery, have you noticed that the tailor's name is sewn onto Fielding's coat?\" [741] \"Let's do it,\" Ellery grinned, pulling out a small notebook from his coat pocket and flipping it open to a blank page. \"Brown Brothers, gentlemen\u2014that's it.\" [742] \"Alright, I'll have Wiley check it out first thing in the morning,\" Quine said. \"You must have realized that Mr. Field's clothing is of high quality. That evening suit would be worth around $300 if bought. Brown Bros. is a renowned tailor, so the price is high. Another relevant point is: all the clothes on the deceased were made by the same manufacturer. For wealthy people, this is nothing special; Brown's would make a complete outfit from head to toe to make the customer stand out. We can be more certain\u2014\"\n[743] \"Mr. Field's hat was also bought there!\" Sampson exclaimed, his face lighting up with a sense of discovery. [744] \"Absolutely correct, Tacitus,\" Quine said, grinning. \"Wiley's job is to verify the clothes, and if possible, get a replica of the same hat Mr. Field wore tonight. I'm eager to see this hat.\" [745] Sampson coughed and stood up. \"I think I should go back to bed,\" he said. \"The only reason I came here was to make sure you didn't arrest the mayor. Buddy, if my friend gets angry, my ears won't be quiet.\" [746] Quine looked up at him, smiling mockingly. \"Before you leave, Henry, tell me your position on this. I know I used high-pressure tactics tonight, but you must have realized it was necessary. Are you going to send someone from your side to assist in the investigation?\" [747] Sampson glared at him. \"When did you start feeling like I'm not satisfied with your investigation? You old fart!\" he roared. \"I've never questioned you, and I don't want to now. If you can't solve this, I don't think anyone else on my side can. My dear Quine, go ahead and do what you need to do. If you need to, you can detain half of New York, and I'll support you.\" [748] \"Thank you, Henry,\" Quine said. \"I just wanted to confirm. Now, since you're so nice, wait and see me do my thing.\" [749] He walked across the room, into the reception area, and called out to the theater from the corridor. \"Mr. Pensel, can you come here for a moment?\" [750] He came back, smiling slightly, as the dark-skinned Pensel followed behind him. [751] \"Mr. Poulson, this is District Attorney Sampson,\" Quinn introduced. They shook hands. \"Now, Mr. Poulson, you have one more thing to do before you can go rest. I want you to close the theater and not let any mice in.\" [752] Poulson's face paled. Sampson shrugged, as if he no longer cared about the matter, and Ellery nodded in agreement. [753] \"But\u2014but officer, we've been having a full house for our performances!\" the short manager moaned. \"Is it really necessary to do this?\" [754] \"Absolutely necessary, my dear fellow,\" the officer replied calmly. \"I'll have two men patrolling around the theater.\" [755] Poulson twisted his hands, casting a glance at Sampson. But the district attorney was turned away, admiring a painting on the wall. [756] \"Oh, that's terrible, Officer!\" Pincus sighed. \"Gordon Davis, the producer, will be on my case about this... But, of course\u2014if you say so, I'll do it.\" [757] \"Ah, don't be so glum, my friend,\" Quinn said, his tone more soothing. \"Through this, your theater's reputation will skyrocket. Once it reopens, you'll have to expand. Anyway, I won't keep your theater closed for too long. I'll give the necessary orders to my men outside, and you can quietly tell them what you need to do when you're done with your daily tasks tonight. You can go home after that. I'll tell you when the theater can reopen in a few days.\" [758] Pincus shook his head sadly and left after shaking hands with them. Sampson immediately turned to Quinn. \"My goodness, Quinn, that's a bit much! Why are you closing the theater? You've searched it top to bottom, haven't you?\" [759] \"But, Henry,\" Quinn said slowly, \"the hat is still missing. We've searched everyone who left the theater, and everyone has only one hat. That means the hat we're looking for must be somewhere here. If it is, I won't let anyone have the chance to come in and take it. I'll be the one to take it if it's here.\" [760] Sampson nodded. As the three of them left the office and entered the nearly empty theater, Ellery frowned with concern. Occasionally, a busy figure could be seen bending over the seats, carefully inspecting the ground. People kept coming and going from the front boxes. Chief Wiley stood by the main entrance, talking in a low voice with Pigott and Haggstrom. Detective Flint was directing some people to work in the front row of the orchestra. A group of tired janitors used vacuum cleaners to clean the theater. In a corner near the back, a plump female officer was talking to an elderly woman\u2014the same woman Pincus had referred to as Mrs. Philip. [761] Quinn and his companions approached the main entrance. Ellery and Sampson silently watched the empty theater, feeling the oppressive atmosphere. Quinn quickly spoke to Chief Inspector Wiley, whispering orders.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"As far as I know, young man, he only has one. How many does a person need?\" [1615] \"You can be sure of that, I think,\" Ellery said. [1616] \"Absolutely, Mr. Quayle,\" she said in a sweet voice. Ellery stared at her as if she were a strange specimen. She pouted and turned away quickly. [1617] \"I'm not very popular here, so I'll be leaving... You're not going to put me in that disgusting jail, are you, officer? Can I go now?\" [1618] The officer bowed. \"Oh, yes\u2014you can go, Mrs. Rathbone, but under certain supervision... but in a few days, we'll need your charming company again, so I hope you'll understand. Can you stay in town?\" [1619] \"Of course, I promise!\" she laughed and strutted out of the room. [1620] Wiley jumped up like a soldier and said, \"Well, I guess that's settled, officer!\" [1621] The officer slumped into the chair, exhausted. \"Thomas, you're like a silly cop in Ellery's novel \u2014 of course, I mean no offense \u2014 suggesting that Morgan was arrested for the murder of Monte Field?\" [1622] \"How \u2014 could there be anything else?\" Wiley seemed bewildered. [1623] \"We'll have to wait a bit, Thomas,\" the old man replied gravely. [1624] Chapter 16: The Quinns Go to the Theater\n[1625] Ellery and his father sat across from each other in the small office. Wiley sat in the chair, frowning in silence for a moment before suddenly seeming to make up his mind to leave the room. [1626] The policeman toyed with the lid of a snuff box, grinning. [1627] \"You're surprised too, Ellery?\" [1628] But Ellery's expression was serious. \"The woman gave me a Woodhouse-style 'creepy feeling,' he said tremblingly. 'Scared' is too mild a word.\" [1629] \"I couldn't quite grasp the meaning of her attitude,\" Detective Quin said. \"I thought she knew, and we were just fumbling around... I was at a loss.\" [1630] \"I think this meeting was quite successful,\" Ellery commented. \"Mostly because I've been collecting interesting facts from this long-winded book about handwriting. But Mrs. Angela Rizzo doesn't fit my idea of the perfect woman...\"\n[1631] \"I'd say,\" chuckled the inspector, \"our fair friend has taken a shine to you. Seize the opportunity, son!\" [1632] Ellery made a face of distaste. [1633] \"All right!\" Inspector Queer picked up the telephone on the desk. \"You think we should give Benjamin Morgan another chance, Ellery?\" [1634] \"He's not worth it,\" Ellery grumbled. \"But it's routine.\" [1635] \"You forgot the documents, son\u2014the documents.\" The inspector's eyes twinkled. [1636] He spoke to the operator at the police station in a cheerful tone, and after a while the phone rang. [1637] \"Good afternoon, Mr. Morgan!\" Quin said jovially. \"How are you today?\" [1638] \"Is this Inspector Quin?\" Morgan hesitated slightly. \"Good afternoon, sir. Has there been any progress in the case?\" [1639] \"That's a good question, Mr. Morgan,\" the policeman laughed. \"But I'm afraid I can't answer that yet, I'm afraid I might be accused of incompetence... Mr. Morgan, are you free tonight?\" [1640] There was a pause on the other end. \"Well\u2014to be exact, no. I'm going home, of course, for dinner, and I believe my wife has arranged for bridge. Anything, Inspector?\" [1641] \"I was hoping to invite you and my son to dinner tonight,\" the officer said regretfully, \"Can you come out for dinner?\" [1642] There was a longer pause. \"If it's absolutely necessary, officer...\"\n[1643] \"It's not absolutely necessary, Mr. Morgan... If you accept the invitation, I would be very grateful.\" [1644] \"Oh.\" Now Morgan's voice was more firm, \"Well, then I'll do as you say, officer. Where shall we meet?\" [1645] \"Great, great!\" Quinn said, \"Six o'clock at Carlos Restaurant, how about that?\" [1646] \"Very well, officer.\" The lawyer replied calmly and hung up the phone. [1647] \"I can't help feeling sorry for the guy,\" the old man murmured. [1648] Ellery muttered incoherently, without sympathy. Angela Lashaw's influence was a thorn in his side. [1649] At exactly six o'clock, Quin and Ellery met Benjamin Morgan in the cozy lobby of Carlos Restaurant. He sat dejectedly in a red leather chair, staring at his hand; his lips drooped with worry, and his legs were subconsciously spread apart due to his gloomy mood. [1650] As the Quin father and son approached, he forced a smile and stood up firmly. This gesture conveyed his determination to the two kind-hearted hosts. The officer was in high spirits, partly because he really liked the plump lawyer and partly because of the official business. Ellery was, as usual, ambiguous in his attitude. [1651] The three men shook hands like old friends. [1652] \"I'm glad you could make it on time, Morgan,\" said the inspector. [1653] A reserved waiter led them to a table in the corner. \"I'm very sorry to pull you away from your meal. Once upon a time\u2014\" he sighed, and everyone sat down. [1654] \"No need to apologize,\" Morgan smiled weakly. \"I suppose you both know that every married man occasionally wishes to attend a bachelor's party\u2014like now. Inspector, do you have something to discuss with me?\" [1655] The old man raised his finger and made a warning gesture. \"Not now, Morgan,\" he said. \"I have an idea: Louis has a talent for cuisine\u2014right, Louis?\" [1656] Dinner was a feast of culinary delights. The officer paid no heed to the subtleties of cooking and handed the menu to his son. Ellery had always been fascinated by food and its preparation. The three enjoyed the meal immensely. Morgan initially tasted the food absentmindedly but grew to appreciate the delicious dishes in front of him, eventually forgetting his worries and engaging in lively conversation with the hosts. [1657] They sipped milk coffee, and Ellery carefully smoked a fine cigar. The officer steered clear of cigars, while Morgan eagerly smoked one. Quin then cut to the chase. [1658] \"Morgan, I don't want to beat around the bush. I'm sure you know why I invited you here tonight. I'll get straight to the point. I want you to explain why you haven't mentioned the events of four days ago, on Sunday, September 23rd.\" [1659] At the officer's words, Morgan's expression grew grave. He placed his cigar in the ashtray and stared at the old man with an indescribable weariness. [1660] \"The inevitable always comes,\" he said. \"I knew you'd eventually find out. I suppose Mrs. Rathbone told you everything in her anger.\" [1661] \"Yes,\" Quin admitted candidly. \"As a man of status, I wouldn't listen to rumors; but as a police officer, it's my duty. Why did you keep it a secret, Morgan?\" [1662] Morgan drew a meaningless pattern on the tablecloth with his spoon. \"Because...uh, because people are idiots unless someone tells them how stupid they are.\" He looked up calmly and said, \"I had hoped and prayed\u2014it's a weakness of human nature, I suppose\u2014that this would be a secret between me and the dead. Discovering that whore hiding in that bedroom\u2014hearing every word I said\u2014made me feel so humiliated.\" [1663] He took a big gulp of water and continued, \"Inspector, I thought I had been set up and couldn't provide any favorable evidence. It was true. I found my most hated enemy murdered at the theater, and it happened just a few steps away from me. I couldn't explain why I was at the scene of the crime unless I told a ridiculous, baseless story; in that moment of agony, I remembered that I had argued with the victim the night before.\" [1664] The inspector remained silent. Ellery leaned back in his chair, frowning at Morgan. Morgan swallowed hard and continued. [1665] \"That's why I didn't say anything. Can you blame someone who, when the law clearly warns him that he is digging his own trap, chooses to remain silent?\" [1666] Quinn paused for a moment before saying, \"Let's not talk about that, Morgan. Why did you go to Field's on Sunday night?\" [1667] \"For a good reason,\" the lawyer said painfully, \"Last Thursday, Field came to my office and told me he needed to make a final business investment and needed to find a way to get fifty thousand dollars immediately. Fifty thousand dollars!\" [1668] \"This is very interesting, Mr. Morgan,\" Ellery said, his eyes flashing. \"Which sentence in the conversation made you suspect he was 'settling old scores'?\" [1669] \"Yes, that's why I said it. He gave the impression that he was short of money and wanted to take a vacation\u2014a trip to Europe for at least three years\u2014he was soliciting funds from 'friends.' I didn't know he was blackmailing on a large scale, but this time\u2014\"\n[1670] Ellery and the inspector exchanged glances. Morgan continued speaking. [1671] \"I told him the truth: I'm not in a good financial situation right now, mainly because of him, and I certainly can't come up with the absurd amount he asked for. He just smiled - insisted on the money. Of course, I was eager to get those documents back...\"\n[1672] \"Did you check the canceled checks to see if those documents were actually lost?\" the officer asked. [1673] \"No, officer,\" Morgan said angrily, \"Actually, he showed me those documents and letters two years ago at the Webster Club - we had an argument. Oh, they're fine. He's a man of his word.\" [1674] \"Go on.\" [1675] \"Last Thursday, he openly threatened me and hung up the phone. In the conversation, I tried to convince him that I would find a way to meet his demands, because I knew that once he realized he had squeezed me dry, he would expose those documents without hesitation...\"\n[1676] \"Did you ask him to show you the documents?\" asked Ellery. [1677] \"I think I did - but he laughed and said he would show me the invoices and letters when he saw the money. He's sly, that con artist - he wouldn't risk me killing him while he was pulling out that damning evidence\u2026 You see, I'm being so honest. I can even admit that the thought of violence occasionally crosses my mind. In that situation, who wouldn't think that? But I never really wanted to kill anyone - there's a good reason for that.\" [1678] \"That doesn't benefit you at all,\" Ellery whispered. \"You still don't know where the documents are!\" [1679] \"That's right,\" Morgan nervously laughed. \"I don't know. Those documents could be exposed at any time - fall into someone else's hands - what good would it do for me if Field died? I might just be trading one bad foreman for another\u2026 On Sunday night, I managed to scrape together money for three days, but to no avail - I decided to come to a final reckoning with him. I went to his apartment, found him in his pajamas, very surprised to see me, but without fear. The living room was a mess - I didn't know Mrs. Rask was hiding in the next room.\" [1680] He lit a cigar with trembling fingers. [1681] \"We argued - to be precise, I argued, and he sneered. He didn't care about any reasons or pleas. He wanted the fifty thousand dollars, or he'd expose the details... and the evidence. After a while, I was furious... I left before I lost control. That's it, officer, I swear it on my honor as a gentleman and an unfortunate victim of fate.\" [1682] He turned his face away. Inspector Quin coughed and threw his cigar into the ashtray. He took a brown snuff box from his pocket, took a pinch, took a deep breath, and leaned back in his chair. Ellery suddenly poured Morgan a glass of water, which he drank in one gulp. [1683] \"Thank you, Morgan,\" Quin said, \"since you've been so candid about the details, would you please tell me honestly if you threatened Field's life during your argument on Sunday night. For fairness' sake, I must tell you that Mrs. Rathbone firmly accused you of killing Field.\" [1684] Morgan turned pale, his eyebrows twitched, and his eyes grew vacant and anxious as he stared pitifully at the officer. [1685] \"She's lying!\" he croaked. Several diners at nearby tables looked around curiously, and Inspector Quin patted Morgan's arm. Morgan bit his lip and whispered. \"I didn't do that, officer. I just told you the truth. I had cruel thoughts of killing Field. It was an impotent, stupid, meaningless idea, and I - I didn't have the guts to kill him. Even when I threatened him in the club, I didn't mean it. Of course, on Sunday night - please believe me, don't believe that shameless, money-hungry whore, officer - you have to believe me!\" [1686] \"I just want you to explain what you said. Because,\" Detective Quin said calmly, \"although it seems strange, I do believe you said what she thought you said.\" [1687] \"What did she say?\" Morgan sweat, his eyes bulging. [1688] \"\u2018Expose those documents if it means destroying me - I guarantee it's your last time blackmailing someone!'\" Detective Quin answered, \"Did you say that, Morgan?\" [1689] The lawyer stared incredulously at the Quin father and son, then burst out laughing. \"Oh my god!\" he gasped, \"That's what she thought was a threat? Officer, I meant that if I couldn't meet his extortionate demands, I would turn myself in to the police and drag him down with me. That's what I meant! She thought I was threatening his life -\"\n[1690] Ellery chuckled, motioned for the waiter to come over, paid the bill, and lit a cigarette, glancing sideways at his father. Detective Quin sympathetically and blankly stared at Morgan. [1691] \"Very well, Mr. Morgan,\" the officer stood up, pushing his chair back, \"That's all we wanted to know.\" He politely turned to the side, allowing the dazed and trembling lawyer to exit first, heading towards the cloakroom. [1692] As the Quinn father and sons strolled down Broadway along Forty-Seventh Street, the sidewalk in front of the Roman Theater was packed with people. The crowd surged, and a barricade was set up to maintain order. The entire narrow passage was at a standstill. The strong lights above the marquee flashed the play's title, \"The Gunfight,\" with smaller lights illuminating the cast list: starring James Parrish, Eve Ellis, and a full cast of stars. Men and women elbowed their way through the throng, police shouted for people to show their tickets to pass the barricade. [1693] The officer flashed his badge, and he and Ellery were pushed and shoved along with the throng of spectators into the theater's small foyer. The manager, Poulson, stood beside the ticket booth with a smile on his Latin American face; he was polite, firm, and authoritative, helping customers move quickly from the ticket booth to the ticket check. The doorman, covered in sweat, stood by, looking bewildered. The cashier worked diligently. Harry Nelson was cornered, engaged in intense conversation with three young men who appeared to be reporters. [1694] Poulson spotted the Quinn father and sons and approached them. Seeing the officer's proud demeanor, he hesitated, nodding knowingly, and returned to the cash window. Ellery stood in line, obtaining two reserved tickets from the ticket booth. They wove through the throng of people, making their way to the front row. [1695] When Ellery handed Maggie O'Connell two tickets clearly marked LL32 Left and LL30 Left, she took a step back in surprise. She awkwardly took the tickets, glancing nervously at the officer, who smiled. She led them down the left aisle to the back row, pointing to the last two seats, and quickly left. They sat down, hanging their hats on the metal racks under the seats, and comfortably leaned back, appearing to be two pleasure-seekers, eagerly anticipating the evening's violent and bloody performance. [1696] The auditorium was packed. Crowds of people lined the aisles, being ushered quickly to their seats by ushers. Many people turned to look at the Quin father and son, who had inadvertently become the center of attention. [1697] \"Damn it!\" the older man complained, \"We should have waited until the curtain went up.\" [1698] \"You're too sensitive to public acclaim, Father,\" Ellery laughed. \"I don't mind being the center of attention.\" He glanced at his watch, and the two exchanged meaningful glances. It was 8:25. They shifted in their seats and settled in. [1699] One by one, the lights went out. The chatter of the audience quieted down. In the darkness, the curtain rose on the stage, revealing a mysterious scene. A gunshot broke the silence; a man let out a gurgling cry, and the audience gasped. \"The Gunfight\" opened in a well-known, exaggerated manner. [1700] Compared to his father's intense concentration, Ellery sat back in his chair, relaxed, and enjoyed the excellent melodrama. James Pierce's deep, resonant voice echoed through the stage, captivating him with the dramatic narrative. Eve Ellis' focus on her role was evident \u2013 she was currently speaking in a low, quivering voice with Stephen Barry. Barry's handsome appearance and pleasing voice prompted the young girl sitting next to the officer to exclaim in admiration. Hilda O'Lanci was squeezed into a corner, dressed in flashy attire, fitting her stage persona. The elderly \"character actor\" aimlessly wandered around the stage. Ellery leaned towards his father. [1701] \"This is a strong cast,\" he whispered. \"Look at that O'Loughlin woman!\" [1702] The performance was full of twists and turns, punctuated by the occasional sound effect, accompanied by the symphony of dialogue and noise. The first act ended. The lights came on, and the detective glanced at his watch: 9:05. [1703] He stood up, and Ellery lazily followed. Marcia O'Connell pretended not to see them, pushing the heavy iron door opposite the aisle. The audience poured out into the dimly lit alley.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nBy the way, the morgue's car should be outside \u2013 I called for one on the way here. Goodbye.\" Plautti yawned and left in a lackluster manner. [368] As soon as Dr. Plautti left, two attendants in white clothes rushed through the carpet with a stretcher. With Officer Quinn's signal, they lifted the motionless body and placed it on the stretcher, covered it with a blanket, and hurriedly carried it away. As the terrible burden was carried away, the agents and police standing by the door breathed a sigh of relief \u2013 the main work of the evening was basically done. The audience rustled, shifted, coughed, and murmured. The sudden departure of the body reignited their interest. [369] Officer Quinn turned to Ellery, sighing wearily. At that moment, there was an unsettling commotion at the back of the theater. The audience stood up from their seats and looked in the direction of the commotion, and the police officers shouted for them to be quiet. Officer Quinn quickly spoke a few words to a policeman standing beside him. Ellery moved aside, his eyes shining. The commotion grew louder, and soon two policemen appeared, dragging a struggling man. They dragged the man to the front of the left aisle, grabbed his arms tightly, and pushed him to his feet. [370] The man was short and had a rat-like appearance. He was dressed in cheap, drab clothing and a shabby black hat that a country clergyman might wear occasionally. His mouth twisted into a grotesque shape and occasionally spewed out vicious curses. However, as soon as he noticed Officer Quinn staring at him, he stopped struggling and hung his head. [371] \"We found this man trying to sneak out through the side door of the theater on the other side, sir,\" one of the policemen panted, not forgetting to push the man roughly. [372] Inspector Quin smiled and took a snuff box out of his pocket, took a deep breath, and habitually sneezed. He smiled at the man who was silent and shrinking between the two policemen. [373] \"Oh, oh, Parsons,\" he said kindly, \"you came just in time, and it's so good.\" [374] Chapter 4 Two Suspects\n[375] Some people are naturally unable to tolerate grumbling men. The crowd was silent under the control of the police, but the man called \"the priest\" was pathetically shouting. Ellery felt disgusted with this man's ridiculous behavior. [376] Erlie watched him with disgust as he noticed the man's ridiculous behavior. Detective Quin's words were clearly sarcastic, and the \"Minister\" stiffened and glared at the officer before trying to break free from the strong arms holding him. He twisted, spit, and cursed, but eventually fell silent. He stopped cursing, but his body was still writhing in anger, and the men holding him were twisting and turning as well. Another policeman joined the fray, pinning the man to the ground. Suddenly, the \"Minister\" deflated like a punctured balloon. A policeman roughly pulled him up, and he stood there, staring at the ground, holding his hat tightly. [377] Erlie turned away. [378] \"Come on, Parsons,\" Detective Quin continued, treating the man like a stubborn child who had just finished a tantrum and finally quieted down, \"you know, this won't work on me. Do you remember what happened last time in the River District? You played the same trick, and how did that turn out?\" [379] \"Ask and answer,\" a policeman growled, poking him in the ribs. [380] \"I know nothing, and I have nothing to say,\" the \"Minister\" mumbled, shifting his weight from one leg to the other. [381] \"I'm surprised at you, Parsons,\" Quinns officer said mildly, \"I haven't asked you what you know yet.\" [382] \"You have no right to arrest an innocent person!\" the \"priest\" shouted angrily, \"I'm as good as the others here, aren't I? I bought a ticket, and I paid with real money. Why won't you let me go home?\" [383] \"So, you bought a ticket, right?\" Quinns officer swayed his body and asked, \"Good, good, then dig out the ticket stub for old Quinns to see.\" [384] The \"priest\" mechanically reached into the pocket under his jacket, his fingers moving quickly inside. He slowly withdrew his hand, but there was nothing. He looked bewildered. He then began searching other pockets, with a look of regret on his face, which made Quinns officer laugh. [385] \"Damn it!\" the \"priest\" grumbled, \"I'm so unlucky. I usually keep the ticket stub, but tonight I threw it away. I'm sorry, officer.\" [386] \"No problem,\" Inspector Quinn said, his face growing stern. \"Enough with the act, delaying us! Kuzinelli, what were you doing at the theater tonight? Why did you suddenly slip away? Tell me.\" [387] The Reverend looked around. His arm was still firmly held by the police, and a group of serious-looking police officers surrounded him. He knew his chances of escape were slim. His expression changed again, feigning a sincere, indignant innocent. Tears blurred his small eyes, like a true Christian martyr, while Inspector Quinn and his men were like pagan persecutors. The Reverend often used this expression to prove his innocence, and it usually worked. [388] \"Officer,\" he said, \"you know, you have no right to question me like this, don't you, officer? Everyone has the right to a lawyer, right? Definitely has the right!\" [389] Inspector Quinn stared curiously at him. \"When was the last time you saw Field?\" he asked. [390] \"Field? You mean Monte Field? I've never heard of him, sir. What are you trying to pin on me?\" the Reverend whispered anxiously. \"What's the charge?\" [391] \"Nothing, Parsons, no charges will be filed. But if you don't want to answer now, I can let you cool off. Maybe you'll say something later... But Parsons, don't forget, there are still some small things to investigate in the Bonomo Silk robbery case.\" [392] Ellery was startled when he heard his father speak as \"the priest\" was being dragged towards the back of the theater. \"This 'priest' is really stupid, isn't he?\" said the officer. \"Trying to slip away like that...\"\n[393] \"Thanks to these little tricks,\" Ellery smiled, \"one mistake can lead to twenty more.\" [394] Officer Quinn smiled and turned around as Wheeler approached with a stack of papers. [395] \"Ah, Thomas is back,\" said Officer Quinn, looking cheerful, \"What did you find, Thomas?\" [396] \"Officer, uh,\" Willie quickly flipped through the stack of records, \"It's hard to say. This is half of the list, the other half hasn't been recorded yet. But I think you might find something interesting.\" [397] He handed Inspector Quinn a stack of papers hastily recording names and addresses. These were the names that Willie had recorded while questioning the audience at the officer's request. [398] The inspector and Ellery, who was standing beside him, began to carefully examine the list, carefully studying each name. Just as they were halfway through, Inspector Quinn suddenly tensed up. He squinted at the name that made him stop, then looked at Willie with some confusion. [399] \"Morgan?\" he said thoughtfully, \"Benjamin Morgan. It sounds familiar, Willie. Have you thought of anything?\" [400] Willie smiled faintly. \"I knew you'd ask me that, officer. Benjamin Morgan was the lawyer partner of Monte Field, and they split up two years ago.\" [401] Quin nodded. The three exchanged glances. Then, Quin shrugged and said succinctly, \"I think we should learn more about Mr. Morgan.\" [402] He sighed and continued to look at the list, studying each name intently. He occasionally looked up, lost in thought, shook his head, and then continued reading. Wiley knew that Officer Quin had an excellent memory, even better than Ellery, so he watched his superior with admiration. [403] Finally, Officer Quin handed the list back to Wiley. \"Nothing there, Thomas,\" he said, his tone serious. \"Did you notice anything I missed?\" [404] Wiley stared at the older man for a moment, said nothing, and simply shook his head before walking away. [405] \"Wait a minute, Thomas,\" Officer Quin called out. \"Before you finish recording the second half, have Mr. Morgan come to the manager's office, please. But don't scare him. By the way, make sure he has a ticket stub before he goes to the office.\" Quin spoke, and Wiley left. [406] As the police were busy completing the tasks assigned by the detectives, Poulzer stood by watching. So, Quinn gestured for him to come over. The short, plump manager hurried over. [407] \"Mr. Poulzer,\" Quinn asked, \"when do your janitors usually start cleaning?\" [408] \"Oh, they've been here for a while, waiting to start, Officer. Most theaters clean up in the morning, but I always have them come in after the evening performance. What's your plan?\" [409] Ellery frowned slightly at Quinn's question but brightened up when he heard Poulzer's response, starting to polish his glasses contentedly. [410] \"Mr. Poulzer,\" Quinn continued calmly, \"what I want you to do is, tonight, after everyone leaves, you arrange for the female janitors to thoroughly clean the place. They must pick up and put away everything \u2013 everything, no matter how insignificant it may seem. Tell them to pay special attention to ticket stubs. Are they reliable?\" [411] \"Oh, absolutely reliable, officer. They've been here since the theater was built. You can trust they won't miss any clues. What should I do with the items they clean out?\" [412] \"Carefully package them, write my name on them, and have a trustworthy person deliver them to headquarters tomorrow morning.\" Detective Quin paused, then added, \"Mr. Pell, I want to emphasize: this task is extremely important, far more so than it appears. Do you understand?\" [413] \"Of course, of course!\" Pell hurriedly left after that. [414] A balding detective strode briskly across the carpet, descending the left aisle, and tipped his hat to Detective Quin. He held a stack of records, similar to those brought by Detective Wiley. [415] \"Detective Wiley asked me to give these to you. They're the remaining audience list and addresses, officer.\" [416] Detective Quinn eagerly took the document from the agent. Ellery leaned forward. The old man slowly scanned the paper with his long fingers, moving from one name to another. As he neared the last page, he smiled, glancing at Ellery with excitement before finishing the page. He turned to his son and whispered a few words. Ellery listened intently, nodding in agreement. [417] Officer Quinn turned back to the waiting detective and said, \"Johnson, come here,\" as he unfolded the paper he had been studying and showed it to Johnson, \"You go find Wells and have him report to me immediately. After that, you go find this woman\u2014\" his finger pointed to a name, along with the corresponding row and seat number, \"and have her come with you to the manager's office. You'll find that a man named Morgan is already there. By the way, if they start talking, listen in\u2014I need to know what they're saying. Treat the lady with respect.\" [418] \"Understood, sir. Johnson also told me,\" Johnson continued, \"that he separated those without tickets and other spectators. He wants to know how to handle them.\" [419] \"Have their names appeared on both records, Johnson?\" Quin asked, handing him the second record to take back to Waley. [420] \"All of them, sir.\" [421] \"Then tell Wheeler to let them leave with the others, but before they go, he should record their names separately. I won't need to see them or talk to them again.\" [422] Johnson saluted and left. [423] Detective Quinn turned to Ellery and spoke quietly. Ellery had some ideas in his mind, but Pendergast's reappearance interrupted them. [424] \"Detective?\" Manager Pendergast coughed politely. [425] \"Oh, what is it, Pendergast?\" Detective Quinn quickly turned around and said, \"Is the cleaning lady doing well?\" [426] \"Yes, sir. Do you have any other orders? And, officer, I beg your pardon, I'd like to ask, how long will the audience have to wait? Many people have asked me this question, and I hope there won't be any trouble on this matter.\" [427] \"Oh, don't worry, Pensel,\" the officer said casually, \"they'll be leaving soon. In fact, I've already ordered my men to let them go in a few minutes. However, before they leave, there's something that will make them complain.\" [428] \"Really, sir?\" [429] \"Yes,\" Quinn said, \"they will be searched. They will undoubtedly protest, and you will hear their threats to sue you or even hit you. But don't worry. I'll take responsibility for everything that happened tonight, and I promise you won't have any trouble... Now, I need a female searcher to help us. We have a female officer here, but she's busy downstairs. You see, can you find a reliable woman for me? - preferably a middle-aged woman - who can accept this ungrateful job and keep it secret.\" [430] The manager thought for a moment. \"I think I can help you. Mrs. Philip, she is our costume custodian. She has been working here for many years and will definitely meet your expectations.\" [431] \"That's her,\" Quin said cheerfully, \"find her and send her to the main entrance. Inspector Willie will give her the necessary instructions.\" [432] Willie, who was walking over, heard the last sentence. Pensel hurried down the aisle, heading for the box. [433] \"Is Morgan ready?\" Quin asked. [434] \"Yes, sir.\" [435] \"Good, then you have one more thing to do, Thomas. I want you to supervise the audience in the main hall and the boxes, making sure they leave one by one. Thoroughly search them before they leave. Everyone must leave through the main entrance, and make sure the guards at other exits direct the audience to the back of the theater.\" [436] Williams nodded. \"Now, regarding the search - Piggott!\" [437] Erlie, who had been leaning lazily against a pillar, stood up and followed Piggott. Williams hurried over, and Quinn called out, \"Don't let the audience upstairs leave yet. Wait until the front row audience leaves before they can go. Send someone up to maintain order and keep them quiet.\" [438] After giving the last important instructions, Quinn turned to the guard nearby, Dole, and whispered, \"Go to the cloakroom, Dole. Keep a close eye on the coat when someone takes it. When they leave, thoroughly search the place. Bring anything left behind to me.\" [439] Erlie leaned against a pillar again, like a marble sentinel standing at the crime scene. He stood there, his eyes glazed over, his hands tightly gripping the collar of his coat. Flint, a broad-shouldered man, rushed over, his eyes shining with excitement. Detective Quinn looked at him cautiously. [440] \"Any discoveries, Flint?\" he asked, fumbling for his snuff box. [441] \"Very good, very good!\" Detective Quinn called out, \"Where did you find it?\" [442] \"Right inside the main entrance,\" Flint replied. \"It looks like the ticket holder dropped it as soon as they came in.\" [443] Detective Quinn didn't speak. His hand suddenly reached into his vest pocket and pulled out the blue ticket stub found on the deceased. He silently looked at them \u2013 two ticket stubs, the same color, one with \"LL32 Left\" printed on it, and the other with \"LL30 Left.\" [444] As he closely examined the innocent ticket stubs, his eyes narrowed into a line. He bent down to take a closer look, carefully aligning the bottoms of the ticket stubs; a hint of confusion appeared in his gray eyes, and then he aligned the tops of the ticket stubs. But no matter how he tried, the two ticket stubs did not match at the tear. [445] Nevertheless, the tearing positions of the two tickets were not consistent with each other. [446] Chapter 5: Interrogating Suspects\n[447] Officer Quinn walked across the wide red carpet laid in front of the theater's main entrance, lowering his hat to cover his eyes. He reached into his pocket, feeling for his indispensable snuff box. Officer Quinn seemed to be deep in thought, as he tightly held the two blue stubs, furrowing his brow in dissatisfaction. [448] He approached a door with green spots, marked \"Manager's Office.\" Before opening it, he turned around to survey the situation. The commotion among the audience had subsided significantly. Chatter filled the theater as police and investigators moved between the rows, issuing commands, answering questions, and ushering the audience out of their seats, lining them up in the main aisle to be searched at the main entrance. [449] Officer Quinn inadvertently noticed that very few people protested the tormenting ordeal. They seemed too exhausted to complain about the rude search. [450] The office's reception area was small, decorated with copper and oak. Parsons Johnny sat slumped in a chair against the wall, puffing away indifferently. A police officer stood next to the chair, a large hand resting on Parsons' shoulder. [451] \"Parsons, come with me,\" Inspector Quinn said, walking forward. The little scoundrel stood up lazily, deftly flicking his cigarette butt into a gleaming copper spittoon, trailing after the inspector with a lackluster air. The police officer followed close behind. [452] Inspector Quinn opened the door to the main office, stepping aside to let the scoundrel and the police officer go first. The door closed behind them with a bang. [453] Louis Posen had a unique taste in office decor. A carved table held a lamp with a transparent green shade. Chairs, ashtrays, a beautifully crafted coat rack, and a long sofa chair, all tastefully arranged in the room. Unlike most managers' offices, Posen did not hang photos of stars, agents, producers, or \"angels\" on the walls but rather hung several exquisite engravings, a large tapestry, and a painting by Constable. [454] However, at this moment, Inspector Quinn was not admiring Mr. Posen's personal office's artistic taste but rather observing the six people across from him. A middle-aged man sat next to Johnson, dressed in an impeccable evening suit, slightly overweight, and with very shrewd eyes, who was now frowning in puzzlement.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThey lifted the seats; the officer surreptitiously took out a long needle from his chest pocket, tentatively poking it into the plush cushion, then knelt down to examine every inch of the carpet with his flashlight. [1463] The two detectives had rolled up the waterproof canvas cover and, under the officer's brief instructions, began to search the boxes, each responsible for one side of the theater. [1464] After a while, the four of them were working in silence, only the officer's labored breathing breaking the stillness. Ellery's work was very efficient, while the old man was slower. After completing a row of search, they met in the middle, exchanged meaningful glances, shook their heads, and continued. [1465] After Poulzer left about twenty minutes ago, the focused officers and Ellery were startled by the ringing of the phone. The sharp and clear ring echoed through the quiet theater. The father and son looked at each other blankly for a moment, then the old man chuckled and hobbled towards Poulzer's office. [1466] Soon, he returned with a smile. \"It's Poulson,\" he said. \"He went to Field's office, but it was locked. No wonder \u2013 it's only 8:45. But I told him to wait until Cronin comes. Won't be long now.\" [1467] Ellery chuckled, and they resumed their work. [1468] Fifteen minutes later, just as the two were about to finish, the front door opened, and a short, middle-aged woman in black stood blinking in the bright arc lights. The officer stood up and went to greet her. [1469] \"Are you Mrs. Poulson?\" he called out kindly. \"It's so nice of you to come so quickly, ma'am. I'm sure you know me \u2013 I'm Quinn.\" [1470] Ellery stepped forward with a rare smile, bowing politely and sincerely. Mrs. Poulson was a likable old woman, short and kind-looking, with silver hair and a friendly demeanor. She immediately won over Quinn's affection for her charming middle-aged appearance, and he had a fondness for mature ladies. [1471] \"I know Mr. Quine,\" she said, extending her hand. \"He was very kind to an old lady on Monday night... I was afraid you'd have to wait for me, sir!\" She turned to the officer and said softly, \"Mr. Poulson sent a message this morning - you know I don't have a phone. I used to be an actress... so I came as soon as I could.\" [1472] The officer smiled broadly. \"That's quite a feat for a lady, Mrs. Philip,\" he said. [1473] \"My father kissed the ground at the feet of the Sphinx, Mrs. Philip,\" Ellery said gravely. \"Don't believe a word he says... I think you'll be able to handle the rest of the front row, Dad? I'd like to chat with Mrs. Philip for a while. Will you be all right with this work alone?\" [1474] \"Of course I will,\" the officer snorted. \"Leave the aisle open, son... Thank you very much for your help, Mrs. Philip.\" [1475] The silver-haired lady smiled. Ellery took her arm and led her to the stage. Quine watched them go, enviously, then shrugged and went back to searching. Soon, when he straightened up, he saw Ellery and Mrs. Philip talking earnestly on the stage, like two actors rehearsing. Quine continued to slowly walk between the rows of seats, looking for clues, but he was still empty-handed when he looked up again. The stage was empty, and Ellery and the old lady were nowhere to be found. [1476] Detective Quinn finally arrived at seat LL32 on the left - it was here that Monte Field met his untimely demise. He carefully examined the cushion, his eyes full of disappointment. He slowly walked through the carpet at the back of the theater, entered Pincus's office. He reappeared shortly, heading towards Harry Nelson's cubicle, the publicity manager. He stayed in the cubicle for a while, then emerged and entered the cashier's office. After closing the door, he descended the stairs on the right side of the theater, leading to the main floor's general lounge beneath the front row seats. He spent some time checking every corner, every recess in the wall, every trash can - he found nothing in any of them. He looked at the water dispenser's large container below with a questioning gaze, then peered inside, finding nothing. He sighed and opened the door with the gold lettering \"Women's Restroom\" and went in. After a while, he emerged and pushed open the double-spring door of the \"Men's Restroom\" and walked in. [1477] After thoroughly checking the lower level, he dragged his heavy steps back up the stairs. In the front row, he found Louis Pincus waiting, his face flushed with fatigue but wearing a victorious smile. The short manager held a package wrapped in brown paper. [1478] \"Did you see Cronin, Pincus?\" Detective Quinn rushed forward. \"You're a real pal, you really are, I can't thank you enough. Is this the package Cronin gave you?\" [1479] \"Yes, Cronin is a good man. He came with two others named Stotts and Loyn shortly after I called him. I waited less than ten minutes. I guess it's important, right, officer?\" [1480] \"Important?\" Detective Quinn said, taking the package from the manager. \"You have no idea how important it is! I'll tell you all about it later... Can I leave you for a moment, Pincus?\" [1481] The small manager's eyes flashed a moment of disappointment, but he nodded. The detective smiled and retreated to a dark corner. Pencroft shrugged and entered his own office. [1482] He left his hat and coat in the office and emerged as the detective was slipping the package into his pocket. [1483] \"Did you get what you wanted, sir?\" Pencroft asked. [1484] \"Oh, yes, not a moment too soon!\" the detective said, rubbing his hands together. \"Now - since Ellery still hasn't shown up - I thought I'd wait in your office until he gets back.\" [1485] They entered Pencroft's private room and sat down. The manager lit a long Turkish cigarette, and the detective produced a snuff box. [1486] \"If I may ask, sir,\" Pendergast said, crossing his short, fat legs and puffing out a cloud of smoke, \"how is the case going?\" [1487] Quin shook his head regretfully. \"Not well\u2014not well. We seem to be making no progress on the main points of the case. In fact, I'll be frank with you, unless we find a lead on something, we're going to fail\u2014it's been a very difficult investigation. He closed the snuff box with a sigh. [1488] \"Too bad, sir,\" Pendergast said sympathetically, licking his lips. \"I had hoped\u2014ah, never mind! I suppose we can't let personal matters interfere with the requirements of justice. If you don't mind telling me, sir, what are you looking for?\" [1489] The policeman smiled. \"Not at all. You've been such a great help this morning, and\u2014oh, I'm an idiot, I should have thought of it before! How long have you been managing the Roman Theater, Pendergast?\" [1490] The manager raised his eyebrows. \"Since it opened,\" he said. \"Before that, I managed the old Electra Theater on 43rd Street\u2014it belonged to Gordon Davis.\" He explained. [1491] \"Oh!\" The officer seemed to be lost in thought. \"So you must be very familiar with the theatre\u2014perhaps you're as familiar with its construction as the architect?\" [1492] \"I am quite familiar with it, yes.\" Pincus admitted, leaning back in his chair. [1493] \"Wonderful! So, here's a little question for you, Pincus... Suppose you wanted to hide something\u2014say, a large hat\u2014somewhere in the theatre, even if you turned the place upside down, you wouldn't find it. What would you do? Where would you hide it?\" [1494] Pincus frowned, pondering his cigarette. \"An unusual question, officer,\" he finally said. \"I'm not sure how to answer. I'm very familiar with the floor plan; I've discussed it with the architect before the theatre was built, and he even sought my input. I can think of many places to hide something as small as a hat, but none of them would withstand a thorough search.\" [1495] \"Understood.\" The officer glanced at his nails, a hint of disappointment on his face. \"So, it's no use. You know, we've searched the whole place top to bottom, and still found no trace...\"\n[1496] The door opened, and Ellery walked in, a little dirty. The officer glanced at him curiously and eagerly. Van Dusen hesitated, clearly wanting the father and son to be alone. The Quinns exchanged a knowing glance. [1497] \"It's okay, Van Dusen \u2013 don't leave,\" said the officer firmly. \"We have no secrets from you. Sit down, old man!\" [1498] Van Dusen sat down. [1499] \"You don't think, Dad,\" said Ellery, sitting at the table and adjusting his glasses, \"that it's time to inform Mr. Van Dusen that the theater can open tonight, as we had decided when he left? Remember, we decided that the theater could open to the public and resume normal performances tonight... \"\n[1500] \"How could I forget?\" said the officer, not blinking an eye, despite hearing this fabricated decision for the first time. \"I think we'll be lifting the ban on the theater soon, Van Dusen. We haven't found anything here, so there's no reason to interrupt your income. You can organize tonight's performance \u2013 in fact, we're eager to see a play, aren't we, Ellery?\" [1501] \"Not quite,\" Ellery said, lighting a cigarette, \"we insist on seeing it.\" [1502] \"Indeed,\" the detective said sternly, \"we insist on seeing it, Poulzer.\" [1503] The manager jumped up from his chair, beaming. \"That's great news, gentlemen!\" he exclaimed, \"I'll call Mr. Davis right away and tell him the good news. Of course -\" his face fell, \"it's too late to expect any reaction from the public tonight. This last-minute notice...\u201d\n[1504] \"You don't have to worry, Poulzer,\" the detective countered, \"I'm the one who closed your door, and I'll make sure the theater is compensated tonight. I'll call the newspaper people and have them advertise the opening in the next edition. That means you'll get unexpected publicity. Without a doubt, it's free advertising, along with the public's natural curiosity, your tickets will sell out.\" [1505] \"You really do things fairly, detective,\" Poulzer said, rubbing his hands together, \"is there anything else I can do for you?\" [1506] \"You forgot one thing, dad,\" Ellery interjected, turning to the dark-skinned, short statured manager, \"can you ensure that the tickets to the left of LL32 and LL30 are not sold tonight? The officer and I want to attend the performance. You know, we haven't enjoyed this pleasure yet. Needless to say, we want to remain anonymous, Mr. Pensler - we don't like the audience's applause and the like. Of course, you will act secretly.\" [1507] \"As you wish, Mr. Queene. I'll have the cashier leave those two tickets,\" Mr. Pensler said, delighted, \"Now, officer - didn't you say you were going to call the newspaper?\" [1508] \"Certainly,\" Queene picked up the phone and spoke briefly with the economic news editor of several city newspapers. After he finished the call, Mr. Pensler bid them farewell and went off to make his own arrangements. [1509] Queene Officer and his son strolled out and took their seats in the front row of the auditorium, where Flint and the two detectives checking the boxes were waiting for them. [1510] \"You stay here as instructed,\" the officer ordered, \"especially today afternoon. Did anyone find anything?\" [1511] Flint frowned. \"I should have gone clam digging in the Nantucket Islands,\" he grumbled, \"I didn't do well on Monday night, and I'm not expecting to find anything today either. The place upstairs was cleaned like a dog's teeth. I think I'll go back and check again.\" [1512] Queene patted the shoulder of the tall detective. \"What's wrong? Don't be like a child, boy. What can you find when there's nothing to find? Did you find anything?\" he asked the other two. [1513] They shook their heads dejectedly. [1514] After a while, the officer and Ellery got into a passing taxi and headed to their headquarters, which was not far away. The old man carefully closed the glass sliding window that separated the driver from the back seat. [1515] \"Hey, son,\" he turned to Ellery, who was lost in thought and smoking, \"explain to your old man what you did in Mr. Pausch's office!\" [1516] Ellery clenched his lips. He stared out the window for a moment before answering. \"Well, let's say this,\" he finally spoke. \"You didn't find anything in today's search, and neither did your men. I've searched carefully too, but I didn't find anything either. Dad, face it: Monte Field was wearing a hat when he went to see 'The Gunfight' on Monday night, and someone saw him wearing it when the second act began. Perhaps the killer took the hat with him when he left, because it's not in the Rome Theater now, and it hasn't been there since Monday night. I'll continue.\" [1517] \"Go on,\" the detective ordered. [1518] \"The second point is simple, even for a child. But allow me to offend your intelligence, Mr. Quinn... If the hat is not in the Rome Theater now, and it hasn't been there since Monday night, then it must have been taken out of the Rome Theater that evening!\" [1519] He paused, looking out the window thoughtfully. A traffic cop was waving his arms at the intersection of 42nd Street and Broadway. [1520] \"So, for three days, we've been exhausted, and we've finally established an important factual basis,\" he continued softly. \"That is, the hat we're looking for has left the Rome Theater... Indeed, it left the Rome Theater on the night of the murder. Now we face a bigger problem - how and when it left.\" He blew smoke and stared at the glowing end of his pipe. \"We know that no one went out wearing two hats or without a hat on Monday night, and no one left the theater disheveled. That is, no one wearing formal attire went out with a felt hat on, and no one wearing casual clothes went out with a silk top hat on. Remember, from this perspective, we didn't find anyone suspicious... Therefore, I conclude that the hat left the theater in the most natural way possible - that is, someone wearing appropriate clothing went out with it!\" [1521] He blew smoke and stared at the glowing end of his cigarette. \"We know that no one went out with two hats or without a hat on Monday evening, and no one left the theater disheveled. That is, no one wearing formal attire went out with a felt hat on, and no one wearing casual clothes went out with a silk top hat on. Remember, from this perspective, we have not found anyone suspicious... Therefore, I must conclude that the hat left the theater in the most natural way \u2013 that is, someone wearing appropriate clothing went out with it!\" [1522] The officer listened intently. He pondered Eretria's words for a moment before saying seriously, \"You've finally given us a lead, son. You said someone left the theater wearing Monte Field's hat \u2013 that's an important and insightful statement. But please answer this: How did he deal with the two hats? Because no one went out with two hats on.\" [1523] Eretria smiled. \"Now you've hit the core of the puzzle, dad. But let's not talk about that for now. We still have many points to ponder. For example, the person who left the theater wearing Monte Field's hat is one of two kinds of people: either he is the killer himself, or he is the accomplice of the killer.\" [1524] \"I understand what you mean,\" the officer mumbled. \"Continue.\" [1525] \"If he is the killer, we have established his gender and that he was wearing evening wear that night \u2013 perhaps not very insightful, as there were dozens of such people in the theater. If he is just an accomplice, then we must conclude \u2013 the killer has two possibilities: either he is a man wearing casual clothes who left wearing a top hat suspiciously, or he is a woman, so she could not possibly wear such a hat!\" [1526] He lowered his voice, and Ellery leaned in. They continued to whisper inaudibly until the taxi stopped in front of headquarters. [1527] Inspector Quin strode forward, and Ellery walked alongside him, as they walked through the dimly lit corridors. They reached his small office, and Inspector Wiley rose slowly. [1528] \"I thought you were lost, Inspector!\" he exclaimed. \"Stotsky was here not long ago, looking all grim. He said Croaning was furious in Field's office - they still couldn't find any evidence in the files.\" [1529] \"Shoo, shoo, Tom, my boy,\" the inspector whispered. \"I don't want to be bothered with trivial matters like locking up dead men. Ellery and I... \"\n[1530] The telephone rang. Quin strode forward, grabbed the receiver from the table. He listened, and the ruddy glow on his thin cheeks faded. His brow furrowed again. Ellery watched him with a strange expression. [1531] \"Officer?\" came a man's urgent voice. \"This is Haggstrom. I have something to report. Just a minute - I can't say too much. I've been tailing Angela Rathbone all morning... it's been quite a task... half an hour ago, she thought she'd shaken me - she got into a taxi and went downtown... and, uh, officer - three minutes ago, I saw her go into Benjamin Morgan's office!\" [1532] Quin barked, \"Keep a close eye on her when she comes out!\" Then he slammed the phone down. He turned slowly to Ellery and Wheeler, repeating Haggstrom's report. Ellery frowned thoughtfully. Wheeler seemed quite pleased. [1533] But the old man sat down weakly in his swivel chair, his voice a little unnatural. Finally, he murmured, \"What do you make of it?\" [1534] Chapter 15: Bringing Charges\n[1535] Detective Haggstrom was a man of calm demeanor. His ancestors had lived in the mountains of Norway, where sluggishness was considered a virtue and serene composure was highly valued.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1] The first part\n[2] Police must follow the albatross's method of operation - knowing that the hands and clubs of the hobos will bring them great misfortune, they still lay their eggs on the beach... police are no exception. All the process of incubation should not be disturbed by any Japanese. [3] Extracted from Yu Chuan's \"Chien Yeh Collection\"\n[4] Chapter 1: Theater Audience and Corpses\n[5] The opening of the 1920s drama festival was embarrassing. Eugene O'Neill forgot to create a new play in time, so he couldn't get financial support from the \"cultured\" people. As for the \"common people\", after watching a few passionless plays, they gave up the formal theater and went to the movie house to find simpler pleasure. [6] On Monday evening, September 24th, a light drizzle covered the brightly lit Broadway theater district from 37th Street to Columbus Circle. Desolate theater managers and directors could be seen everywhere. Several plays were canceled on the spot by higher-ups, hoping that God and the weather bureau would witness their plight. The rain kept people who should have been at the theater at home by their radios and card tables. Only a few brave souls ventured out into the deserted streets in the rain. Broadway was indeed in a gloomy mood. [7] However, on the sidewalk in front of the Roman Theater on 47th Street, west of \"White Street\", there was a crowd of spectators who only came out during the mid-period of the theater festival when the weather was fine. The signboard of \"The Gunfight\" shone brilliantly under the decorative awning of the theater entrance. The cashier skillfully served the crowd of people waiting in front of the \"Today's Performance\" window. The doorman wore a sober uniform of yellow and blue, his demeanor refined by the passage of time, and was therefore very conspicuous. He bowed to lead the evening's audience into the front row of the auditorium. These spectators wore high hats and leather coats, with satisfied expressions on their faces, as if nothing could stop them from watching \"The Gunfight\" performance. [8] In the latest theater on Broadway, people were hurriedly looking for their seats, obviously a little anxious, as the play's noisy characteristics were well known. At this point, the last chattering audience stopped, the last audience to arrive was tripped by his neighbor, and the lights gradually dimmed, and the curtain slowly rose. A gunshot echoed, a man screamed... the show began. [9] \"The Gunfight\" is the first play of the drama festival to use the characteristic sound effects of the mob. Automatic pistols, machine guns, attacks on nightclubs, mob killings that kill enemies with loud noises - all the clich\u00e9d ways of romanticizing the mob society were woven into the fast-paced three-act play. It exaggeratedly reflected the era - a little vulgar, a little lowbrow, but all in all, it satisfied the audience who watched the play. Therefore, whether it was sunny or rainy, the play was always full and sold out. The situation in the theater tonight was enough to prove its popularity. [10] The performance progressed smoothly. The first act reached its climax with an explosion, and the audience shuddered. The rain had stopped, and people used the ten-minute break after the first act to step out of the theater and breathe in the fresh air after the rain. Then, the curtain slowly rose, and the second act began. The explosions on the stage were even louder. With the sharp-tongued dialogue behind the stage lights, the second act quickly entered the crucial moment. The slight commotion at the back of the theater did not attract attention, as it was normal in the noise and darkness. It seemed that no one noticed anything was wrong, and the plot continued to unfold with a bang. However, the noise of the disturbance gradually increased. At this point, several audience members in the back row on the left began to stir in their seats, angrily defending their rights. This protest was highly contagious, and soon dozens of eyes turned to the front row of the auditorium. [11] Suddenly, a piercing scream echoed through the theater. The audience, engrossed in the rapid events unfolding on stage, craned their necks expectantly in the direction of the scream, hoping for a new dramatic twist in the plot. [12] The theater lights suddenly snapped on, illuminating bewildered, terrified, and enraptured faces, all satisfied with the plot. Near the leftmost closed exit, a tall policeman stood, grabbing the arm of a slightly tense-looking man. The policeman pushed away a crowd of inquisitive people with a loud voice, shouting, \"Stay in your seats and don't move!\" [13] The audience burst into laughter. [14] But the smiles quickly faded. As the audience began to realize that even the actors looked confused and hesitant, despite continuing to recite their lines behind the stage lights, occasionally casting puzzled glances at the front row of the auditorium. People stood up from their seats, uneasy in the face of the anticipated tragedy. The officer's authoritative voice continued to roar, \"I said, sit still! Stay where you are!\" [15] The audience suddenly realized that this was not a play but reality. Women screamed, desperately clutching their companions. The upper-level audience was in a panic, despite not being able to see what was happening downstairs. [16] The officer turned sharply to a small, dark-skinned man dressed in evening attire, standing nearby and fidgeting with his hands. [17] \"Mr. Pensler, I need you to close every exit immediately and make sure they are all locked,\" he barked. \"Assign a usher to each entrance to prevent anyone from entering or leaving. Have someone guard the alley until the police arrive. Move quickly, Mr. Pensler, or there will be trouble.\" [18] Some excited audience members disregarded the officer's loud warnings and crowded around to ask questions. The small, dark-skinned man pushed them away and hurried off. [19] The officer spread his legs and stood at the last exit on the left, using his massive frame to block a person lying on the floor between two rows of seats. The person was dressed in evening attire and lay in an odd position. The officer looked up, grabbed the trembling man beside him, and quickly scanned the back of the auditorium. [20] \"Hey, Nelson!\" he called. [21] A tall, blond-haired man quickly emerged from the small room next to the main exit, pushing through the crowd and approaching the policeman. He looked sharply at the motionless body on the floor. [22] \"What happened, Dohr?\" he asked. [23] \"You'd better ask this guy,\" the policeman said sternly, shaking the arm he was holding. \"Someone died, and this one\u2014\" he glared fiercely at the huddled little man, \"Bosack, William Bosack. Mr. Bosack here says,\u201d Dohr stammered, \"Mr. Bosack says he heard the dead man whisper that he was murdered.\" [24] Nelson stared at the body, dumbfounded. [25] The policeman bit his lip. \"This is a real pain in the ass, Harry,\" he croaked. \"I'm the only cop here, and I have to deal with a bunch of hysterical idiots... I need your help.\" [26] \"Sure, whatever you say\u2014this is quite a mystery.\" [27] Doris suddenly turned around and shouted at the man sitting three rows ahead who was staring at him, \"Hey, get down here! And you guys, back to your seats! Now! Anyone who meddles gets a punch from me!\" [28] He turned to Nelson. \"Harry, get back to your office and call headquarters. Tell them there's a murder here. Have them send a squad over\u2014bring a lot of them. Tell them it's at the theater\u2014they'll know what to do. And, Harry\u2014take my whistle and blow it outside. I need help.\" [29] Nelson walked back through the crowd, and Doris called after him, \"Harry, make sure they send old Quin over.\" [30] The man with light brown hair disappeared from view, returning to his office. Soon, a sharp whistle echoed through the sidewalk in front of the theater. [31] The dark-skinned theater manager followed Doral's orders to have people guard the exits and alleys, and then he rushed back through the crowd. His evening dress shirt was wrinkled, and he wiped his forehead, looking flustered. As he turned to go forward, a woman stopped him. [32] She said sternly, \"Mr. Pensler, what gives this policeman the right to keep us here? I tell you, I have the right to leave! I don't care what's going on here - it's none of my business - it's your business - tell him not to do this stupid thing of locking up innocent people!\" [33] The little man tried to escape while stammering, \"Oh, ma'am, please listen to me. I'm sure this officer knows what he's doing. Someone was killed here - it's serious. You don't see... I'm the theater manager, and I have to follow his orders... please calm down... be patient...\"\n[34] He slipped away from her before she could object. [35] Doral waved his arms frantically and shouted from his seat, \"Everyone, sit down and be quiet! I don't care if you're the mayor, you - the one with glasses - sit down, or I'll take action! Don't you know what's going on? Be quiet, I said!\u201d He jumped down from the seat, wiping the sweat from the brim of his hat and muttering to himself. [36] The main floor was filled with agitation and panic, like a boiling pot. People on the upper levels stretched their necks over the railings, trying to figure out the cause of the commotion below, but to no avail. The audience had forgotten that the performance on stage had been interrupted. Actors mumbled lines from the script by the stage footlights, but the words had lost their meaning. The curtain slowly fell, and the evening's performance ended. The actors chattered nervously as they hurried down the steps. They, like the audience, stared in confusion at the scene of the incident. [37] Among these actors, the plump and radiant elderly lady named Hilda Orlancy, an excellent guest actress, was portrayed as \"Barmaid Murphy\" on the poster; the delicate and elegant \"street urchin Nettie\" was played by Eve Ellis, the female lead; James Peir, the sturdy and tall male lead in \"The Gunfight,\" wore a tweed suit and a hat; Stephen Barry, the young man, looked smart in his evening wear, playing the role of an upper-class youth falling into the \"gangster\" trap. Lucy Hallton's portrayal of the \"streetwalker\" attracted harsh criticism from drama critics during this inappropriate drama festival, as they had little else to criticize or accuse. The elderly man with a pointed beard wore a flawless evening gown, proving that M. Ler Blond, the costume supplier for \"The Gunfight,\" was a remarkable tailor. As the bulky villain looked down at the chaotic audience, his anger on stage disappeared, and a rare gentleness appeared on his face. In fact, all the theatrical personnel, wearing wigs and makeup, rushed down the stage steps, running slowly under the falling curtain, rushing down to the front row of the main floor, and pushing their way through the aisles towards the scene of the disturbance. [38] At the main entrance of the theatre, another commotion made people disregard Dahl's order to stay put and stand up to see what was going on. A group of people in blue uniforms pushed their way in, holding batons. Dahl sighed deeply and saluted the tall man in plainclothes who led them. [39] \"What's going on, Dahl?\" the newcomer asked, frowning at the chaos around him. The blue-uniformed men pushed the audience back to the seating area in the front row. Standing people tried to sneak back to their seats, but were caught and then pushed to the back row, joining the angry crowd. [40] \"Report, Chief, this person seems to have been murdered,\" Dahl said. [41] \"Ah, ha,\" the plain-clothes man glanced indifferently at the motionless body lying at their feet, its face covered by a black-sleeved arm, legs awkwardly spread out, extending under the front row chairs. [42] \"What happened - a shooting?\" the newcomer asked Doyle, turning his eyes. [43] \"No, sir - it doesn't seem to be,\u201d the officer replied. \"I immediately had a doctor from the audience come and check - he thinks it's poisoning.\" [44] The police chief grumbled, \"Who is this?\" He pointed to Poussac, shivering beside Doyle, and suddenly shouted loudly. [45] \"The one who found the body,\" Doyle answered. \"He hasn't left the scene.\" [46] \"Good,\" the detective turned to the crowd of people crammed a few feet behind him and asked, \"Who is the manager here?\" [47] Poulson stepped forward. [48] \"I'm Wiley, the chief of detectives from headquarters,\" the plainclothes man growled. \"Didn't you make these idiots quiet?\" [49] \"I did my best, Chief,\" the manager rubbed his hands and mumbled, \"but they seem to be angry at the policeman's actions,\" he pointed apologetically at Doyle, \"he shouted at them. I don't know how to get them to sit down as if nothing had happened.\" [50] \"Oh, we'll handle it,\" Wiley interrupted him and quickly gave orders to the uniformed men by his side. [51] \"Hey \u2013\" he turned to Dorr and called, \"how about the doors and exits? Have you taken any measures?\" [52] \"Of course, Inspector,\" the policeman grinned, \"I've had Mr. Panzer assign ushers to guard every entrance. They've been there all night. But I've also specifically instructed them to ensure that everything is secure.\" [53] \"Good. Has anyone tried to leave?\" [54] \"I can assure you, Inspector,\" Panzer interjected, \"that we need ushers almost at every exit for this play. It's a crime drama with lots of gunshots, screams, and so on. Having someone at every door enhances the mystery. I can easily check for you if \u2013\"\n[55] \"We'll handle it ourselves.\" Wheeler said, \"Dorr, have you asked anyone to come over?\" [56] \"I called Inspector Quinn,\" Dorr replied, \"I had this publicist Nelson call headquarters.\" [57] Wiley's cold face cracked a slight smile. \"You're quite thorough. And what about this body? Has anyone touched it since this guy found it?\" [58] The man, tightly held by Dorr and huddled up, cried, \"I \u2014 I just found him, sir \u2014 I swear to God, I \u2014\"\n[59] \"Alright, alright,\" Wiley said coldly, \"Are you going to cry all night? What's there to cry about? Hmm, Dorr?\" [60] \"No one has touched it since I arrived,\" Dorr answered, a hint of pride in his voice, \"Of course, except for Dr. Stutgart. I had him called from the audience to confirm that the man was dead. No one else has come close.\" [61] \"You've been busy, Doyle?\" Wiley said. He whirled around to face Pensler, who stepped back. \"Mr. Manager, you'd better go up on stage and announce that everyone stays put until Officer Quinn says they can leave\u2014understand? Tell them to cooperate, the more they cooperate, the sooner they'll be out of here. And make it clear that they stay in their seats, anyone who causes trouble is a suspect.\" [62] \"Yes, yes. My God, what a mess!\" Pensler grumbled as he made his way down the aisle towards the stage. [63] Just then, a small group of people pushed through the stage door at the back of the theater and stepped onto the carpet. [64] Chapter 2: One Quinn Works, Another Quinn Observes\n[65] Detective Richard Quinn was an unremarkable man in both form and demeanor. The elderly gentleman was short and gaunt, with a kindly, placid face. He walked with a slight stoop but moved with a measured pace; he had a full head of white hair and a thick beard, a pair of gray eyes that sparkled with intelligence, and long, slender hands that seemed to complement his demeanor perfectly. [66] As Quine walked quickly across the carpet, eyes from all directions fell upon him, but he left no lasting impression. However, his refined and imposing aura was unique, and his wrinkled face broke into a warm smile, causing a murmur among the audience, which blended perfectly with the rhythm of his progress. [67] The police's changes were more obvious. Doyle retreated to the corner of the left exit. Wiley, the Chief Inspector, stood calmly next to the corpse - while everyone else was almost hysterical, he remained mocking and indifferent. He seemed to willingly give up his central position. The police guarding the aisle saluted the inspector. The tense, grumbling, and angry audience inexplicably relaxed and sat back heavily. [68] Inspector Quine walked up to Wiley and shook his hand. [69] \"It's really a pity, Thomas. I heard about it when you were about to go home.\" He whispered. He smiled at Doyle like a loving father and then glanced at the man on the ground with a hint of pity. \"Thomas,\" he asked, \"are all the exits guarded?\" Wiley nodded. [70] The old man turned around and scanned the surroundings of the scene with interest. He asked Wiley a question quietly, who nodded in agreement; then he beckoned Doyle over with his finger. [71] \"Doyle, where are the people sitting in these positions?\" He pointed to the three chairs next to the corpse and the four chairs in the front row. [72] Doyle looked puzzled. \"I don't see anyone, Inspector... ...\"\n[73] Quinn stood silently for a moment, gesturing for Doyle to return, and then whispered to Wiley, \"It's a crowded room... Do you remember the case?\" Wiley frowned solemnly. \"I feel that this case is a bit tricky,\" the inspector continued in a gentle tone, \"What I can see now is a dead man and a group of sweaty people making a commotion. Let Hessey and Piggott direct the crowd, hmm, child?\" [74] Wiley shouted for the two plainclothes officers who had entered the theater with him. The two men turned around, struggling to squeeze their way to the back, pushing people aside. The police joined the two detectives in their action. The actors were ordered to retreat. The police cordoned off the middle row of seats with a rope, and about fifty people squeezed into the narrow space. The police quietly surrounded them, instructing them to show their tickets and then returning to their seats one by one. Within five minutes, all the audience had returned to their seats. The actors were told to stay within the rope for the time being.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."